2600:
** κατά-βᾰσις**, εως, ἡ,
opposed to ἀνάβασις, **way down, descent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186 (_plural_), 7.223, etc.}}; ἡ εἰς Ἅιδου κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.20, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.12, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.122}}; title of work by Dicaearchus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.31.2}}; compare καταίβασις.
**2** **descent from** central Asia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.4}}; ἡ ἐπὶ θάλατταν κ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.25.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **descent** of an idea into the mind, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.242.}}
**4** **steep ground, declivity**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 248 (_plural_).}}
**5** ἔργον καταβάσεως **hanging** work, {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.7.16(29).}}

2601:
** καταβῐβ-άζω**,
causal of καταβαίνω, **make to go down, bring down**, τινὰ ἀπὸ τῆς πυρῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87, compare 86; τοὺς ἐκ τοῦ καταστρώματος ἐς κοίλην νέα [prev. author] 8.119; στρατιώτας.. εἰς τὴν Χώραν τῶν Φρυγῶν “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 16.3; τὴν πόλιν πρὸς τὴν θάλασσαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 4}}; **bring from town to country**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cam.” 10}}; down into a mine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.86, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.262d}}: _metaphorically_, **bring down, lower**, κ. σεαυτὸν ἀπὸ αὐχημάτων εἰς τὸ δημοτικώτερον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.45}}:—_passive_, κωμῳδία -βιβασθεῖσα εἰς τὸ λογοειδές {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.6.}}
**2** **force to come down**, εἰς τὸ ὁμαλὸν τὸ στρατόπεδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.6.7, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65}}; **drive away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.143.}}
**II** **bring down**, τὴν διήγησιν ἐπὶ τὴν ἀρχὴν τοῦ πρώτου Φοινικικοῦ πολέμου {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.8; τὸν λόγον ἐπὶ τὰ νῦν καθεστῶτα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 20.}}
**III** **bring down** the accent, i.e. **throw** it **forward**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 213.16, “Etymologicum Magnum” 774.34.}}
**IV** Astron., ὁ -βιβάζων (i.e. σύνδεσμος) the **descending node**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 30.6, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 5.101.}}

2602:
** καταβολ-ή**, ἡ,
**throwing down**: hence, **sowing,** {{Refs|Corp.Herm. 9.6}}; especially of **begetting**, κ. σπέρματος, σπερμάτων, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 13, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 19, cf. NT.Heb.11.11, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.13.3}}; ἡ Ῥωμύλου σπορὰ καὶ κ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.320b.}}
**b** **congenital defect**, ἀπὸ ξυγγενικῆς αἰτίας καὶ κ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 37.}}
**c** Astrology texts, **nativity**, ἡ ἐξ ἀρχῆς κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 220.29, +others}}
**2** **paying down**, especially by instalments, καταβάλλειν τὰς κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.27}}; τὸ ἀργύριον ἔφερε καταβολὴν τῇ πόλει paid money as **a deposit** (by way of caution),{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Docum.[prev. work] 37.22, compare “IG” 12(7).515.26 (Amorgos, 2nd c.BC), “UPZ” 112v12 (_plural_, 2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; ἔχειν τῆς γῆς.. καταβολήν **liability for rent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 23.17 (3rd c.BC)}}: _plural_, **instalments,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLips. 12.17 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **laying of a foundation**: hence, **building, structure**, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.2Mac.2.29; τῆς ἀρχιτεκτονίας 3rd-2nd c.BC: Bito Mechanicus 49.2; ἔργου 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.2.9: but usually _metaphorically_}}
**II.1** **foundation, beginning**, ἱερῶν ἀγώνων {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.4; τῆς περιόδου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 352b15; κ. ἐποιεῖτο καὶ θεμέλιον ὑπεβάλλετο τυραννίδος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.6.2; κ. κόσμου NT.Matt.13.35, NT.Eph.1.4; κ.κοσμική “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).138 (Thessal.); ἡ πρώτη κ. τῆς φιλοσόφου θεωρίας 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus}} **in Alc.Praef.**p.8C.; ἐκ καταβολῆς from **the foundations**: hence, **anew**, σκάφη ἐκ κ. ἐναυπηγοῦντο, of **fresh** construction, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.36.8}}; ἐκ κ. πλάττων, of **pure** invention, {{Refs|[prev. author] 15.25.35}}: hence, of **set purpose, deliberately**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.47.7, 24.8.9.}}
**II.2** ={θυσία},{τελετή}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, compare κατηβολή.}}
**III** **periodical attack** of illness, **fit**, τῆς ἀσθενείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 519a, compare κατηβολ; πυρετοῦ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.29, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.399, 2.563, compare 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 50(26).59, [prev. author] 2.166J.}} **trance**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.16}}; cf. Latin _catabolicus._
**IV** **detraction, abuse**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.56S.: _plural_, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.571 codices}}
**V** perhaps **outer wrapper** {{Refs|compare (κατάβλημα 11.4)}} of a bandage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 9.}}

2603:
** καταβρᾰβεύω**,
**give judgement against** one **as** βραβεύς, and so, **deprive** one **of the prize, deprive** one **of one's right**, with _accusative_, {{NT+8th c.BC+|NT.Col.2.18, Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.399:—}}_passive_, ὑπὸ Μειδίου καταβραβευθέντα **being cast in his suit** by means of Meidias, Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.93, compare “Sammelb.” 4512 B 58 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** with _genitive_, **rule over**, ἡ γῆ κ. τῶν λοιπῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 344.29.}}

2605:
** κατ-αγγέλλω**,
**announce, proclaim, declare**, πόλεμον κατηγγέλκασι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.30, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.68, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 26; κ. ῥύσιά τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.53.2; δεῖπνον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.727b: frequently in “NT”, κ. τὸ εὐαγγέλιον, Χριστόν, NT.1Cor.9.14, NT.Phil.1.17: with}}_infinitive_, κ. ἱκέσθαι **report** one's arrival, {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.170 (from Cyrene).}}
**2** **recite, recount**, ἀγῶνα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 39.}}
**3** **denounce**, τινὸς τὴν ἐπιβουλήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.38}}; δοῦλοι ὅσοι δεσπότας κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.2.2}}; **lay an information**, πρός τινα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3641 b. 32 (from Lampsacus): _metaphorically_, κ. ἀπειρίαν τοῦ ποιητοῦ 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 78.20.}}
**4** of symptoms, **threaten**, σπασμόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.2.6.}}

2606:
** καταγελάω**,
_future_ **-άσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.9}}, late **-γελάσω** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} see at {κατακριδεύσει}:— _passive_, _future_ **-γελασθήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 22}}: _perfect_ **-γεγέλασμαι**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 1.1}}: _pluperfect_ κατεγεγέλαστο {{Refs|[prev. author] “Icar.” 19}}:—**laugh, jeer at**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.68, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1081, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482d: also with _dative_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.37, 4.79, +others}}: _absolutely_, **laugh scornfully, mock**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 372 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 161, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.151; ἅπαντες καταγελῶσιν, ὅταν τις.. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 28.9; ἐπί τινι 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 22.272b.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **laugh down, deride**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 286, LXX.Sir.7.11}}:—_passive_, **to be derided**, ὑπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1271, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 680}}; καταγελάμενος (_Doric dialectpresent_ _participle_ _passive_) ὑπὸ τῶν ἄλλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 4.951.123 (Epid.); τὸ εὔηθες καταγελασθὲν ἠφανίσθη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83; τὸ καταγελᾶσθαι γὰρ πολὺ αἴσχιόν ἐστι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Epit.fragment” 7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3c, +others}}

2607:
** καταγιγνώσκω**,
_Ionic dialect_ and later **καταγηρ-γῑνώσκω**, _future_ **-γνώσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2b}}:—**remark, observe**, especially something **to one's prejudice**, with _genitive_ _person_:
**I** generally, καταγνοὺς τοῦ γέροντος τοὺς τρόπους **having observed** his foibles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 46}}; πολλήν γέ μου δυστυχίαν κατέγνωκας I have been very unfortunate **by your way of it**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25a; πολλὴν ἡμῶν ἐρημίαν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.2}}; οὐκ ἐπιτήδεα κατά τινος κ. **having formed** unfavourable **prejudices** against one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.97}}: with _infinitive_, of an unfavourable judgement, κ. ἑαυτοῦ μὴ περιέσεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45, compare 7.51; αὐτὸς ἐμαυτοῦ κατέγνων μὴ ἂν καρτερῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.36, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 19d: followed by ὅτι, ὡς, ἐμοῦ κατέγνωκας ὅτι εἰμὶ ἥττων τῶν καλῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76c; οὐκ ἂν καταγνοίην ὑμῶν οὐδενὸς ὡς.. ἀμελήσετε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.4 (but κατεγνωκότες ὅτι.. ἐφθείρομεν}} **despising** us because.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34, compare “PMagd.” 42.4 (3rd c.BC), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.108b)}}: with _participle_, κ. τινὰ πράττοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 2.18, compare “Cyr.” 8.4.9; τὸ Χωρίον νοσερὸν [ὂν] καταγνόντες 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.109}}:—_passive_, **to be judged unfavourably, lightly esteemed**, παρολιγωρεῖσθαι καὶ καταγινώσκεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.27.6}}; κατεγνωσμένος **despised**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.29.}}
**II** with _accusative_ criminis, **lay as a charge against** a person, κ. ἑωυτῶν ἀνανδρείην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 22; κ. τινὸς μηδὲν ἀνόσιον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12}}; δειλίαν, δωροδοκίαν κ. τινός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.16, 21.21; οὐδὲν ἀγεννὲς ὑμῶν καταγιγνώσκω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.152; ἑαυτῶν ἀδικίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.3}}; πολλὴν μανίαν, μωρίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.133, 5.21; σκληρότητα ἡμῶν καὶ ἀγροικίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607b; τοσαύτην ὑμῶν εὐήθειαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.38: with _genitive_ understood, οὐ γὰρ ἐκεῖνό γε (i.e. σοῦ) καταγνώσομαι, ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2b; later κ. κατά τινος τὸν φόνον 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.30:}} —_passive_, καταγνωσθεὶς δειλίαν **being convicted** of cowardice, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.22; κ. ἐπὶ λογοκλοπίᾳ 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.54; κατεγνωσμένος}}**self-condemned,** {{NT|NT.Gal.2.11.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ criminis, παρανόμων κ. τινός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.67; παρανοίας ὑμῶν αὐτῶν [prev. author] “Prooem.” 35: with _accusative_ _person_, κ. τινὰ φόνου}} **pronounce a verdict** of murder **against.. **, Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.30}}; μὴ καταγιγνώσκωμεν τὸ (perhaps τοῦ) μηδὲν εἰρηκέναι τὸν ἀποφηνάμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 206e.}}
**II.3** with _infinitive_, κ. σφῶν αὐτῶν, ἑαυτοῦ ἀδικεῖν, **charge** oneself **with.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.6, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.6, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.175, 206; κ. ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.78:}} —so in _passive_, καταγνωσθεὶς νεώτερα πρήσσειν **being suspected** of doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.2}}; κ. αὐθέντης (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.11}}; **to be detected**, ἔν τινι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 175.16 (3rd c.AD)}}; also κατέγνωσται μελίκρητον ὑπὸ τῶν ἀνθρώπων ὡς καταγυιοῖ τοὺς πίνοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 56.}}
**II.4** with _genitive_ _person_ only, **condemn**, τοῦ ἀνθρώπου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Demodocus” 382e.}}
**III** with _accusative_ poenae, **give judgement** or **sentence against** a person, κ. τινὸς θάνατον **pass sentence** of death **on** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60}}; Μηδισμοῦ κ. τινὸς θάνατον **for** Medism, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.157; κ. τινὸς φυγήν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.106; φυγὴν αὑτοῦ καταγνούς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.38}}: with _infinitive_, κ. αὐτοῦ ἀποτεῖσαι τὰ Χρήματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.18}}; later θάνατον, φυγὴν κ. κατά τινος, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.62, 19.51}}:—_passive_, θάνατός τινος κατέγνωστο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.70, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.39, Jusj. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.149; later καταγνωσθεὶς θανάτῳ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.49: _absolutely_, κατεγνώσθησαν}} they **were condemned**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.8; τὸ ἀδίκημα κεκριμένον ἐστὶ καὶ κατεγνωσμένον 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 52.}}
**III.2** **decide** a suit, δίκην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1360}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 573 codices; δίκη μὴ ὀρθῶς -γνωσθεῖσα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.3.}}

2608:
** κατ-άγνῡμι**,
_infinitive_ -ύναι [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 265e}}; **καταγνύω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 107.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.5}}; late _present_ **κατάσσω**, **κατεάσσω** (which see): _future_ κατάξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 323}}: _aorist_ κατέαξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc. (see. below)}}; _Ionic dialect_ κατῆξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.26}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ κατάξει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 38.37 (Teos, 5th c.BC)}}; _participle_ κατάξας (Dobree for κατεάξας) [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.42, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.526b (variant{κατεάξας},{κατάγξας})}}]; _Epic dialect_ _optative_ καυάξαις ={καϝϝάξαις} for κατ-ϝάξαις, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 666, 693}}:—_passive_, κατάγνῠμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 45, “Art.” 67, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 703}}: _imperfect_ κατεάγνυτο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 113G.:}}_aorist 2_ κατεάγην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[probably ᾰ] 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1428}}_subjunctive_ κατ-ᾱγῶ (_contraction_ from κατᾰ-ϝᾰγ-) {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 604, probably in [prev. author] “Ach.” 928, _optative_ κατᾱγείην [prev. work] 944}}; _participle_ καταγείς [probably ᾱ] {{Refs|“IG” 2.1673.33, 39, +others, later κατᾰγέντος “APl.” 4.187}}: _future_ καταγήσομαι{{Refs|“Cat.Codex Astr.” 8(4)}}.129: _perfect_ κατέᾱγα, _Ionic dialect_ κατέηγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 67 (in _passive_ sense)}}; _participle_ κατεαγώς, written κατειαγώς {{Refs|“IG” 22.1673.55, _contraction_ κατηγώς}} Phoenix5.1: _perfect_ _passive_ κατέαγμαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 10, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.46.5, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 5.32}}: _aorist 1_ κατεάχθην {{LXX|LXX.Jer.31(48).25}}; _infinitive_ καταχθῆναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 640a22}}; _participle_ καταχθείς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 26.52, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 11.82.--The forms κατέαξα, κατεάγην}} led the copyists to insert the ε in unaugmented forms, as κατεάξας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [same place], κατεαγῇ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 50, κατεαγῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469d}}, and such forms were in use in later Gr., as κατεάξει {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.12.20, κατεαγῶσιν NT.John.19.31, κατέαξαν “BGU” 908.25 (2nd c.AD)}}:— **break in pieces, shatter**, κατά θ᾽ ἅρματα ἄξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.403; ἄξονα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 693}}; τὸ (i.e. ἔγχος) γὰρ κατεάξαμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.257; νέα μέν μοι κατέαξε Ποσειδάων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.283, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 666; εἴ τινες μαχεσάμενοι ἔτυχον ἀλλήλων κατάξαντες τὰς κεφαλάς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.42; κατάξειέ τις αὐτοῦ μεθύων τὴν κεφαλήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1166}} codex R (variant{τῆς κεφαλῆς},{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare οὐ γὰρ κατάξει τῆς κεφαλῆς τὰ ῥήματα 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 323, κατῆξε τῶν πλευρέων 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.26, see entry near the end)}}; κατάξω τὴν κεφαλήν, ἄνθρωπέ, σου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 173; γυνὴ κατέαξ᾽ ἐχῖνον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1436; Ναξίαν ἀμυγδάλην κατᾶξαι 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista Comedy texts 68; τὰς ἀμυγδαλᾶς.. κάταξον τῇ κεφαλῇ σαυτοῦ λίθῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 590}}: _metaphorically_, **break up** into species, μὴ κ. μηδὲ κερματίζειν τὴν ἀρετήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 79a.}}
**2** **weaken, enervate**, πατρίδα θ᾽, ἢν αὔξειν Χρεὼν καὶ μὴ κατᾶξαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 508; τὰς ψυχὰς καταγνύουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.5: _absolutely_ in}}_perfect_ _participle_ κατεαγώς **effeminate**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 18, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.524f}}; αὐλητὴς τῶν κ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 4; κ. μουσική 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 6.14.}}
**II** _passive_ with _perfect_ _active_, **to be broken**, δόρατα κατεηγότα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.224; ὀστέα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 8; κληΐς [prev. author] “Art.” 14; περὶ δ᾽ ἐμῷ κάρᾳ κατάγνυται τὸ τεῦχος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 565.3; κατέαγεν ἡ Χύτρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 403}}; especially καταγῆναι τὴν κεφαλήν **have** one's head **broken**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.61, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.14; τὴν κεφαλὴν κατεαγέναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.35: Comedy texts, στάμνου κεφαλὴν κατεαγότος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 545; τὸ κρανίον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 684; τὸ σκάφιον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 604}}; κατεαγέναι or κατάγνυσθαι τὰ ὦτα, of pugilists, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 515e, “Prt.” 342b; τὴν κλεῖν κατεαγώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.67: also with _genitive_ _partitive_ (οὐ πᾶσαν τὴν κεφαλὴν ἀλλὰ μέρος τι αὐτῆς 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Philet.” p.448 P.), τῆς κεφαλῆς κατέαγε περὶ λίθῳ πεσών 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1180}}; κατεάγη τῆς κ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 1428}}; τῆς κ. καταγῆναι (-εαγῆναι, -εαγέναι codices) δεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469d; κατέαγα τοῦ κρανίου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 48}}: _metaphorically_, **to be shattered**, of an argument, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus [same place]}}

2609:
** κατ-άγω** [ᾰγ],
_future_ **-ξω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26, etc.}}: _aorist_ κατήγᾰγον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.164, 4th c.BC: Epigenes Comicus 7: rarely}}_aorist 1_ κατῆξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.20, “PGrenf.” 2.44 (2nd c.AD), 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 10.4}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _infinitive_ **-αξέμεν**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.53:}}_perfect_ καταγήοχα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.73}}:—**lead down**, especially **into the nether world**, ψυχὰς μνηστήρων κατάγων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.100; εἰς Ἀΐδαο 11.164, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.34, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.6.2}}: generally, **bring down** to a place, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.163; τὴν ἐκ τῶν ὀρῶν ὕλην εἰς τὸ ἄστυ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118d, etc.}}; **bring down** a river or canal, {{Refs|“PGrenf.” [same place]}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 708.3 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **draw down**, κατῆγεν ἦγεν ἦγεν ἐς μέλαν πέδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1065}}; especially by magic arts, κ. τὸν Δία {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 15; ἀετόν [prev. work] 8}}, uncertain meaning in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.9.4.}}
**3** **bring down to the sea-coast**, κατάγειν κοίλας ἐπὶ νῆας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.26, compare 6.53; ἐπὶ θάλατταν τὸ στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.18.}}
**3.b** **launch**, σκάφος εἰς τὴν θάλασσαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 1.}}
**4** **bring down from the high seas to land**, τὸν Κρήτηνδε κατήγαγε ἲς ἀνέμοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.186}}: _absolutely_, **put in to shore**,{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3.10 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus (κατάγοντο codices)}}; especially for purposes of exacting toll or plundering, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.33, “An.” 5.1.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.25, +others; κ. ναῦς ἐς τοὺς ἑαυτῶν συμμάχους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.28; also κ. τοὺς ἐμπόρους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.95.4, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.82}}; κ. σαγήνην **draw** it **to land**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 4}}; τοῦ πνεύματος κλύδωνα κατάγοντος πολύν **bringing in** a heavy swell **from the sea**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mar.” 36; ὥρα πνεῦμα λαμπρὸν ἐκ πελάγους κατάγουσα [prev. author] “Them.” 14}}:—_passive_, **come to land, land**, opposed to ἀνάγεσθαι, of seamen as well as ships, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.178; ἐπ᾽ ἀκτῆς νηΐ κατηγαγόμεσθα 10.140, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.43; Σίγειον οὐρίῳ πλάτῃ κατηγόμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 356; κατάγεσθαι ἐς τὸν Μαραθῶνα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.107, compare 8.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 240c; εἰς τὸν λιμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.36.}}
**4.b** κατάγεσθαι παρά τινι **turn in and lodge** in a person's house, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 344, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.39, “PFlor.” 248.11 (3rd c.AD)}}; ὥς τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.22; εἰς οἰκίαν [prev. author] 49.22; εἰς πανδοκεῖον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.773e.}}
**5** **draw down** or **out, spin**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 46, 4th c.BC: Epigenes Comicus 7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 226b}}; κατάγουσα, ἡ, **spinning-girl**, statue by Praxiteles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 34.69: _metaphorically_, κ. λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 80e.}}
**6** **reduce** to a state, ἐς κίνδυνον φανερὸν κ. τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.68}}; ὁ οἶνος εἰς ὕπνον κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 13.6.}}
**7** **bring home, gain**, θρίαμβον καὶ νίκην τῇ πατρίδι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.33.7; ἐκ πολέμων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 24}}; **escort**, ἐπὶ τιμητείαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aem.” 38, etc.}}
**8** κ. γένος **derive** a pedigree, ἀπό τινος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.843e, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus 61 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus:}} —_passive_, τὰ στέμματα κατάγεται εἴς τινα **are traced down** to.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 1; φᾶμαι κατάγοντο 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 1.39}} P.; of persons, **to be descended**, ἀπό τινος {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “Vita Platonis;” p.1 W.}}
**9** **derive** a word, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.242 (_passive_)}}: generally, **derive**, ὅθεν δεῖ κατῆχθαι καὶ πῶς ἀποδεικνύειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.203S.}}; κ. [βοάν] **lower** the voice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 149 (Lyric poetry)}}: _metaphorically_, **bring down, lower**, πρὸς αὑτόν to one's own standard, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 40.11.}}
**10** in Medicine texts, **couch** a cataract, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).680.}}
**11** **wind up** a torsion-engine, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 76.13}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 79.6; ὁ κατάγων τὴν Χεῖρα 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 75.9.}}
**12** καταγόμενος **current**, ἐνιαυτός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 27.16.}}
**II** **bring back**, κατὰ δὲ φρόνιν ἤγαγε πολλήν **brought back** much news [of Troy], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.258}}; especially from banishment, **recall**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 647, 660, etc.; κ. οἴκαδε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.2}}: generally, **restore**, τυραννίδας ἐς τὰς πόλις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. ; εἰς τὰς πατρίδας.. εἰρήνην 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.105.2; ἐκ ταλαιπωρίας 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.58c}}:—_passive_, **return**, ἐπὶ τὸ στρατόπεδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.36.}}

2610:
** κατᾰγων-ίζομαι**,
**prevail against**, τινας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.42.3, +others, “OGI” 553.7 (from Xanthus); τὰ αἰσχρὰ τῶν παθῶν 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.19; κ. Ὀδυσσέα περὶ στεφάνου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.22; ἕλκη διαίτῃ 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “Vita Isidori” 122}}:—_passive_, καταγωνισθῆναι τὰ ὅλα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.4.12; ὑπό τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 19.}}
**2** **contend against**, τὴν ἀλήθειαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.5.5, compare 12.25d. 6.}}
**II** **win by a struggle**, βασιλείας {{NT|NT.Heb.11.33.}}

2611:
** καταδέω**
**A**, _future_ -δήσω, **bind on** or **to, bind fast**, πρυμνήσια, ἱστόν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.436 (tmesis), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.425 (tmesis); ἵππους μὲν κατέδησαν.. ἱμᾶσι φάτνῃ ἐφ᾽ ἱππείῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.567; ἐπ᾽ ἀμβροσίῃσι κάπῃσιν 8.434; ἐμὲ μὲν κατέδησαν.. ἐνὶ νηΐ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.345; κ. λάρνακας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.123}}:—_passive_, καταδεδεμένος τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.122; ἐν φόβῳ καταδεθεῖσα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1498 (Lyric poetry) (so μανίη καταδεῖ τινα 4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.85); καταδεῖται ψυχὴ ὑπὸ σώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 83d; γλῶττα -δεδεμένη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 492b32}}:—_middle_, **bind to oneself**, ἀγχόνιον βρόχον κατεδήσατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 687 (Lyric poetry); σπόγγους περὶ τὰ ὦτα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 960b15: _metaphorically_, ἀριθμῷ καταδήσασθαι}} **tie up for oneself** in lots, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 11.3; καταδησαμένη τινὰ ὁρκίοις 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 12.3.}}
**A.b** κ. τι ἀπό or ἔκ τινος, _metaphorically_, **establish securely**, τὴν διὰ πάντων διήκουσαν ὠφέλειαν ἀπὸ [τοῦ συλλογισμοῦ] {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Alcibiadem” p.252 C., compare 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus in “de An.” 15.34.}}
**A.2** **bind up**, θραῦμα, τραύματα, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.27.21, NT.Luke.10.34.}}
**A.3** **put in bonds, imprison**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.143, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 70e, etc.}}; κ. τὴν ἐπὶ θανάτῳ (i.e. δέσιν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72.}}
**A.4** **convict of** a crime, opposed to ἀπολύω, with _infinitive_, κ. τινὰ φῶρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.174, compare 4.68.}}
**A.II** **tie down, stop, check**, ἀνέμων κατέδησε κελεύθους or κέλευθα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.383, 10.20; ὅς μοι ἐφορμήσας ἀνέμους κατέδησε κελεύθου 7.272, compare 4.380; τοῦ γε θεοὶ κατὰ νόστον ἔδησαν 14.61.}}
**A.III** **bind by spells, enchant** (with _future_ -δήσομαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3), 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator _Attic dialect_ 6.7 (_passive_), “SIG” 1175.2 (4th-3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; κ. τὸ ἐργαστήριόν τινος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Tab.Defix.” 71.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; κ. τινὰ γλῶτταν καὶ ψυχὴν καὶ λόγον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Tab.Defix.Aud.” 49.1 (4th-3rd c.BC)}}; γοητεῦσαι καὶ κ., of {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Cleopatra, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.5}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Tab.Defix.” 107a2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 38, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.378f}}; compare καταδηνύω, καταδίδημι.

2613:
** καταδῐκ-άζω**,
**give judgement** or **sentence against** a person, **condemn**, opposed to ἀποδικάζω:—Constr.: with _genitive_ _person_ et _accusative_ _of things_, κ. τινὸς θάνατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.45}}; τὴν διπλασίαν (i.e. ζημίαν) Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.105; πολλὴν μοχθηρίαν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.24; πολλὴν τὴν ἀπόγνωσιν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 11}}: with _genitive_ _person_ et _infinitive_, κ. σεαυτοῦ τὰ ἔσχατα παθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.9, compare “An.” 6.6.15: with _genitive_ _person_ only, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 29.2: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 958c; τᾶν ψάφων ταὶ -δικάζουσαι “SIG” 953.83 (Calymna, 2nd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, **get sentence given against** a person, δίκην καταδικάσασθαί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.49, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.176}}; κ. τινός, without _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.14, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.18}}; κ. τινὸς Χρημάτων **get** a person **condemned** [to a payment] of money, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.3.7: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 857a, “PHal.” 1.65 (3rd c.BC), etc.:}} —_passive_, καταδικασθείς **condemned**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 958c; ὑπὸ ἐχθρῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.51}} W.; ἐπὶ φόνῳ **for** murder, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.76; ἐπὶ κακουργία [prev. author] 3.12}}; later καταδικασθῆναι θανάτῳ **to be condemned** to death, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 13.101, etc.; θάνατον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 68.1; τὴν ἐπὶ θανάτῳ 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4.60; φυγήν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰταλική” 3; εἰς ἀλαβαστρῶνα “Sammelb.” 4639.2 (3rd c.AD)}}; εἰς δοῦλον, εἰς νῆσον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4.65, 5.21}}: with _infinitive_, καταδικάζεται ἀποθανεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.29}}; of the sentence, ἀντέλεγον μὴ δικαίως σφῶν καταδεδικάσθαι **that judgement had been given against** them unjustly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.49; later -δικασθείσης αὐτοῦ δίκης “PHal.” 1.44 (3rd c.BC),compare “PLille” 29.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; of fines imposed, τὰ -θέντα ἐκπραξέω {{Refs|“IG” 22.1126.5 (Amphict. Delph.).}}
**II** _middle_, **have judgement given in one's favour**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.9, 10.24.}}
**III** **declare by express judgement**, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.21.}}
**IV** _passive_, **to be bound by** a law,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Men.Prot. p.39 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

2614:
** καταδῐώκω**,
**follow hard upon, pursue closely**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.49, 2.84, LXX.Psa.17(18).38, “PCair.Zen.” 439 (_passive_, 3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.29}} W., etc.: _metaphorically_, **try to gain**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.42.1.}}
**2** **search for**, τινα {{NT|NT.Mark.1.36.}}
**3** **overdrive** cattle, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.33.13.}}

2615:
** καταδουλ-όω**,
**reduce to slavery, enslave**, Ἀθήνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109; τὴν Ἑλλάδα [prev. author] 8.144; Ἀθηναίοις κ. Κέρκυραν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; νῆσον βασιλεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.20}}:—_passive_, κατεδεδούλωντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.116; κατεδουλώθησαν [prev. author] 6.32; καταδεδουλωμένος ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219e: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.5.}}
**2** more frequently in _middle_, **make a slave to oneself, enslave**, τὴν μητρόπολιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.51, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 351b}}; τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.13, compare “GDI” 4982 (from Gortyn), “PEleph.” 3.3 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; ἡ τύχη τὸ σῶμα κατεδουλώσατο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 95.8}}; τὸ κρέσσον τῷ Χείρονι -εύμενοι (_Ionic dialect_ for -ούμενοι) {{LXX+4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Eusebius Myndius Philosophus “fragment” 10; κ. τὸν Ἰσραὴλ δουλείαν LXX.1Mac.8.18}}; ἔργα ὧν κατεδουλοῦντο αὐτούς{{LXX|LXX.Exo.1.14.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **enslave in mind**, παιδισκάριόν με καταδεδούλωκ᾽ εὐτελές {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 338, cf. NT.2Cor.11.20; κ. τὴν ψυχήν “PMag.Lond.” 123.4 (4th-5th c.AD)}}; **break in spirit**, καταδουλοῖ τὴν τόλμαν ἡ ἀνάγκη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 81.}}
**II.2** more frequently in _middle_, ἡ ἀνάγκη καταδουλοῦται τὴν γνώμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 15}}; οἴει τι μᾶλλον καταδουλοῦσθαι ἀνθρώπους τοῦ ἰσχυροῦ φόβο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.23, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1269; κ. τὰς ψυχάς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.178; τὸ λογιστικόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553d; τὰς ἐπιθυμίας 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Fragmenta Historica” 15.}}

2616:
** καταδῠναστ-εύω**,
**oppress**, τινα {{LXX|LXX.Exo.1.13, +others}}; τοὺς πτωχοὺς ἀπὸ τῆς γῆς{{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Amos.8.4: _metaphorically_, δέδοικα μὴ πλοῦτός με -εύσῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.8}}; τινος {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.73, NT.Jam.2.6: _absolutely_, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.1.26, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.421, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.367d}}:—_passive_, **to be oppressed,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.74 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Neh.5.5, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 37.8; ὑπό τινος 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.4; ὑπὸ τοῦ διαβόλου NT.Act.10.38}}; ταῦτα -εύετο ἕκαστα these districts **were under their** several **rulers**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.7.8.}}
**2** **get control**, _absolutely_, of mutineers, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Centil.” 56.}}

2617:
** καταισχύν-ω**,
_future_ **-αισχῠνῶ**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author = 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus] “Th.” 546}}:—**dishonour, put to shame**, μή τι καταισχύνειν πατέρων γένος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.508; καταισχύνητέ τε δαῖτα 16.293; τὰ πρόσθε ἐργασμένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.53, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 996, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.101, etc.}}; τὴν σὴν οὐ κ. φύσιν I **put** not thy nature **to shame**, i.e. show myself not unworthy of thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 609; κ. τὸ Τρωϊκὸν κλέος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 845; τὸ γένος οὐ καταισχυνῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1451; κ. τὴν πατρίδα [prev. author] “Nu.” 1220; τοὺς προγόνους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187a; ὑποσχέσεις [prev. author] “Smp.” 183e; τὰς εὐγενείας ταῖς αὑτῶν.. κακίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.76, etc.}}
**2** **dishonour** a woman, ἀλλοτρίας γυναῖκας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.49}}; also of a male, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.79.}}
**3** ὁ μέλλων Χρόνος ἐμὸν καταίσχυνε.. Χρέος **covered** me **with dishonour** in that my debt remained unpaid, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).8.}}
**4** ={καταχέζειν},{Χαίτην} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 82.8.}}
**II** _middle_, **feel shame before**, θεούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1382, compare “OT” 1424}}: —_aorist_ _passive_, καταισχυνθέντες τὴν ἀρετὴν αὐτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.97}}: with _infinitive_, **to be ashamed** to.., ἰητρεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 42}}; καταισχυνθῆναι.. ὅπως μὴ δόξει.. **to be ashamed** of being thought.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.13.}}

2618:
** κατακαίω**,
_Attic dialect_ **κατακάω** [ᾱ], _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ κατακαιέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.408}}: _future_ **-καύσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1218}}: _aorist_ κατέκαυσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κατέκηε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.418}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_ κατακήομεν (variant{-κείομεν}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.333}}; _infinitive_ κατακῆαι [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.46, κακκῆαι [prev. work] 74 (variant{-κεῖαι})}}]: _perfect_ **-κέκαυκα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.37, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.69M.}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-καυθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1505}}, **-καήσομαι** {{NT|NT.1Cor.3.15:}}_aorist_ κατεκαύθην (the _Attic dialect_ form) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.69, 6.101, κατεκάην [prev. author] 1.51, 2.107}}; _Laconian dialect_ _infinitive_ **-καῆμεν**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 20}}; -εκαύσθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Chron.Lind.” 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41}}: _perfect_ **-κέκαυμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.108}}:—**burn completely**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} of sacrifices and dead bodies, κατακήομεν αὐτούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.333; μιν κατέκηε σὺν ἔντεσι 6.418}}; κ. τοὺς μάντιας **burn** them **alive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.69; ζῶντα κατακαυθῆναι [prev. author] 1.86, compare 2.107}}; of cities and houses, etc., κατὰ μὲν ἔκαυσαν.. πόλιν {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.33; κατεκαίετο ὁ ἐν Δελφοῖσι νηός [prev. author] 1.50}}; [οἰκίη] κατεκάη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.79; κατακαυθέντων ἱρῶν [prev. author] 6.101; τείχη -κεκαυμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator}} [same place]; γῆ κατακεκαυμένη **burnt** earth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 358a14}}; Κατακεκαυμένη, name of the upper valley of the Hermus, in Lydia, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.4.11, compare κατακεκαυμενίτης}}; of the fingers, **to be burnt** with hot food, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.15; also κ. τὴν κοιλίαν “PMagd.” 33.4 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** of hot winds, **parch**, τὰ ἐκ τῆς γῆς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.27.73 (3rd c.BC), +others}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ὁ ἔρως ἐμέ.. κατακέκαυκεν {{Refs|“Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 8}} (**c**):—_passive_, τὰ στόματα -κάεται ἐπὶ τέχνην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 33.6; -καίομαι καταλελειμμένη “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 1.24.}}
**II** _passive_, of fire, κατὰ πῦρ ἐκάη **had burnt down, burnt out**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.212.}}

2621:
** κατάκειμαι**,
_Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ κατακείαται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.527}}: _Ionic dialect_ _pluperfect_ κατεκέατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.229}}; _subjunctive_ **-κέωμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 213b:}} —_passive_, only in _present_ and _imperfect_ with _future_ _middle_ -κείσομαι:—**lie down**, μῆλα τὰ δὴ κατάκειτ᾽ ἐσφαγμένα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.532}}; ἐπὶ πλευρὰς κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.10}}; νέκυς κ. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.19; ἐφ᾽ ἁρμαμαξῶν μαλθακῶς κατακείμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 70.}}
**2** **lie hid**, ἐν λόχμῃ.. κατέκειτο μέγας σῦς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.439; θάμνῳ ὑπ᾽ ἀμφικόμῳ κατακείμενος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.677.}}
**3** **lie stored up**, δοιοὶ γάρ τε πίθοι κατακείαται ἐν Διὸς οὔδει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.527; τό γ ἐν οἴκῳ κατακείμενον ἀνέρα κήδει 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 364: _metaphorically_, ἄλγεα.. ἐν θυμῷ κ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.523.}}
**3.b** **to be deposited**, of deeds in a registry, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1040.32 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**4** **lie sick, keep one's bed**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.229, NT.Mark.1.30, etc.}}; **lie in bed**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 313; ἐφ᾽ ὃ κατέκειτο NT.Luke.5.25.}}
**5** **lodge, reside**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.έ, 3.1.γ, +others}}
**6** **to be idle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.14}}; of things, **lie neglected**, καθεύδειν ἐᾶν ἐν τῇ γῇ κατακείμενα τὰ τείχη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 778d.}}
**7** **recline at meals**, πῖνε, κατάκεισο {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 985, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.121, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 185d, NT.Mark.14.3.}}
**8** of land, **lie sloping to the sea**, πρῶνες ἔξοχοι -κεινται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.52.}}
**9** ἀρετᾷ κατάκειται πᾶσαν ὀργάν **is expended** in every impulse **on**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “I.” 1.41.}}

2622:
** κατακλάω** [ᾰ],
**B**_imperfect_ κατέκλων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.227, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.62}}: _aorist 1_ **-έκλᾰσα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117d}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ and _aorist_ (see. below):—**break short, snap off**, ἐπ᾽ ἀνθερίκων καρπὸν θέον οὐδὲ κατέκλων Il.[same place]; κατεκλάσθη δ᾽ ἐνὶ καυλῷ ἔγχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|13.608; τὰ δόρατα κατέκλων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.34; φυτευτήρια ἐλαῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.15}}; κατὰ δ᾽ αὐχένα νέρθ᾽ ἐπὶ γαίης κλάσσε **bowed** it **down**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.146}}; κ. τὸν ὀφθαλμόν **ogle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.79 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; but ὄμματα κατακεκλασμένα eyes **with drooping lids**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 808a8; τὸ σῶμα.. -κέκλασται}}**has been crushed,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 77.12 (6th c.AD).}}
**B.II** _metaphorically_, **break down**, οὐδένα ὅντινα οὐ κατέκλασε he **broke** us all **down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” [same place]; πάθος, εἴτ᾽ οἶκτος εἴτ᾽ αἰδώς, κατέκλασε τὴν διάνοιαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 7}}; [Ἔρως] κατακλάσας τὸ σοβαρόν {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.767f}}:—more frequently in _passive_, ἐμοί γε κατεκλάσθη φίλον ἦτορ, κλαῖον δ᾽ ἐν ψαμάθοισι καθήμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.538}}; of fear, ἡμῖν δ᾽ αὖτε κατεκλάσθη φίλον ἦτορ δεισάντων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|9.256, compare 10.198; τὸ θράσος κατεκέκλαστο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 11}}; of passion, ἐρώτων.. νόσῳ φρένας.. κατεκλάσθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 766 (Lyric poetry)}}; of pity, οὐδὲ κατεκλάσθης {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 107}}; of persuasion, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.114.}}
**B.II.2** _passive_, κατακεκλασμένος **reduced by fever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 510}}: _metaphorically_, of character, **to become enfeebled, degenerate**, {{Refs|Aristeas 149: in}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, **enervated, effeminate**, of men, {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 339.2}}; γραφαὶ κ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 18:}} —_active_, κ. ἑαυτόν, of an effeminate dancer, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 18, “Salt.” 27.}}
**B.III** _passive_, of light, **to be refracted**, opposed to ἀνακλᾶσθαι (to be reflected), ὄψεως -κλωμένης {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 3.18.1}}; of sound, αἱ κατακλώμεναι φωναὶ μετὰ φαρμακείην **broken, feeble** voice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 246.}}

2623:
** κατα-κλείω**,
old _Attic dialect_ **κατακλῄω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus (see. below): a rare}}_future_ κατακλιῶ uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 287, compare 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 107.13}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ **-εκλεισάμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.5:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ -εκλῄσθην, -εκλείσθην (see. below); _Ionic dialect_ **-εκληΐσθην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.128}}; _Doric dialect_ **-εκλᾴσθην**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.84}}: _perfect_ **-κέκλῃμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 206.}}
**I** with _accusative_ _person_, **shut in, enclose**, e. g. a mummy in its case, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86: frequently of blockading, τοὺς Ἕλληνας ἐς τὴν νῆσον κ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.109; κ. ἑαυτοὺς εἰς ἔρυμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.18; κατακλείειν τοὺς γυμνῆτας εἴσω τῶν ὅπλων [prev. author] “An.” 3.4.26}}; κ. εἰς πολιορκίαν, εἰς δυσχωρίας, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.74, 11.26; κ. τινὰ ἐν φυλακῇ NT.Luke.3.20, compare “OGI” 669.17 (Egypt, 1st c.AD): _metaphorically_, κ. ἑαυτὸν εἰς πολιτείαν}}, i.e. not to be a cosmopolite, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.13}}:—_passive_, ἐς τὸ τεῖχος κατακλῄεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.57; ναυσὶ κατεκλῄσθησαν [prev. author] 1.117, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.7}}; ὅταν ἐς [νεφέλας] ἄνεμος κατακλῃσθῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 404; εἰς μικρὸν τόπον -κεκλῃμένοι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.34; διὰ τοῦ ζῆν.. κ. ἐν Ἀπόλλωνος ἢ Ἀθηνᾶς 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.17}}:—_middle_, **shut oneself up**, ἐν τοῖς βασιλείοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.5}}; also κατεκλᾴζετο **shut up** the bride **with oneself** [in the bridal-chamber], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.5}}:—_passive_, κατεκλᾴσθης {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.84.}}
**I.2** _metaphorically_, νόμῳ κ. **shut up**, i.e. **compel, oblige**, ἂν.. πᾶσαν τὴν δύναμιν νόμῳ κατακλείσητε ἐπὶ τῷ πολέμῳ μένειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.33, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.7, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 190.15.}}
**I.3** _metaphorically_, τῆς πόλεως εἰς κίνδυνον μέγιστον κατακεκλειμένης **being reduced**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.11; εἰς σπάνιν κατακλεισθῆναι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.74}}: generally, **confine**, ἐν τῷ κατὰ φύσιν πέρατι -κέκλειται τἀγαθόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.8; πᾶσαι αἱ ἐπιχειρήσεις εἰς μίαν ἀπόδειξιν -κλείονται 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.283 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; κατακλείειν τὸ πᾶν τῆς τέχνης εἰς.. **confine** the whole business of art to.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 3.4.}}
**II** c._accusative_ _of things_, **shut up, close**, τὰς πυλίδας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.191; τά ἱρά [prev. author] 2.124, compare 128 (_passive_)}}; τὸ ἐργαστήριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.14; τὸν δίφρον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.10; εὑρὼν ἅπαντα κατακεκλῃμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 206}}:—in _passive_, of humours in the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 27.}}
**II.2** **clamp down, make fast**, of stones in masonry, {{Refs|“IG” 7.3073.158(Lebad.)}}; also κ. [τὴν δεξιάν] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 2.}}
**II.3** **close** a speech, **conclude**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 10.138}}; εἰς ἀπειλὴν κ. τὸν λόγον with a threat, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.14, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 234.17}}; οὐ κ. διάνοιαν **give** no **complete** sense, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Adv.” 119.6 (δ. should be supplied, [prev. author] “Synt.” 179.13)}}; **conclude** an argument or inference, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 15, 33.}}

2625:
** κατα-κλίνω** [ῑ],
**lay down**, [δόρυ] κατακλίνας ἐπὶ γαίῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.165}}; κ. τοὺς Πέρσας ἐς λειμῶνα **having made** them **recline** (for dinner) in a meadow, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363c, 420e, NT.Luke.9.14, “Milet.” 1(9).368}}; κ. παιδίον **put** it **to bed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 19, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 3; κ. τινὰ ἐν ἁρμαμάξῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.11}}; also, **cause** one **to take to his bed**, i.e. **strike with disease,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.2075}}; of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.3}}; κ. τινὰ εἰς Ἀσκληπιοῦ **lay** a sick person in the temple of Asclepios, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 411, “V.” 123}}; ταύταν ὀβολῶ κ. (obscene meaning) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 5.31:}} —_passive_ (with _aorist 2_ _Attic dialect_ -εκλίνην, _aorist 1_ -εκλίθην _Attic dialect_ and in other dialects), **lie at table**, κατακλιθέντας πίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; κατακλῐνήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 98, compare “V.” 1208}}; generally, **lie down**, κατακλινεὶς δευρί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 694}}; κατακλίνεσθαι παρά τινα **lie** at table **next** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175a}}; but, παρά τινι **lie with** him sexually,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 203c; κατακλίνηθι μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 904}}; κ. ἐπὶ ταῖς κοίταις, ἐπὶ στιβάδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1040, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.15}}; of a sick man, **take to one's bed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.2}}; simply, **lie in bed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prog.” 3, Diocl.fragment 141; κατακλιθέντα ἐς τὸ ἱερόν 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 18; κατεκλίθη ὕπτιος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117e}} codices; κατακεκλιμένος, of a corpse, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.53.1.}}
**II** **cause to incline, bend downwards**, ἕως ἂν κατακλίνῃ [ὁ ἐλέφας τοὺς φοίνικας] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610a23}}: _metaphorically_, **lay prostrate, overthrow**, τύραννον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1181.}}
**III** _passive_, of ground, **slope**, ἤπειρόνδε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.734.}}
**III.2** of the sun, **set**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.157.}}
**III.3** of crabs' eyes, **turn sideways**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 529b28.}}
**III.4** **kneel**, ὅταν κατακλιθῇ εἰς γόνατα [κάμηλος] {{Refs|[prev. work] 499a17.}}
**III.5** with _dative_, **to be set under, made subject to**, ὅταν κατακλιθῇ τὸ θητικὸν τῷ προπολεμοῦντι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.157 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}

2626:
** κατα-κλύζω**,
_future_ -κλύσω [ῠ], _poetry_ **-κλύσσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).10}}: _perfect_ κατακέκλυκα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMagd.” 28.10 (3rd c.BC)}}:—**deluge, inundate**, τὴν γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13 (of the Nile), compare 99 (_passive_), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.50, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 22d, “OGI” 90.24 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.15 [= 3 p.xv] (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; ὑπ᾽ ὄμβρων -κλυζόμενος {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.12; κόσμος ὕδατι -κλυσθείς NT.2Pet.3.6.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **deluge, overwhelm**, τοίους γὰρ κατὰ κῦμα.. ἔκλυσεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 9.3; τὴν Φρυγῶν πόλιν.. ἤλπισας κατακλύσειν δαπάναισιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 995; ἅπαντα.. κατακλύσει ποιήμασιν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 186}}; κ. ἀφθονίᾳ δίαιταν **make** life **overflow** with plenty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 2.8}}; κατακλύσαι δεινῶν πόνων **deluge** with sufferings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 343 (Lyric poetry); εἰ καὶ μέλλει γέλωτι.. ὥσπερ κῦμα.. κατακλύσειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473c:}} —_passive_, ἀλλοδαπῶν κύματι φωτῶν -κλυσθῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|of a city, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1084 (_anapaest meter_); -κλυσθεὶς ὑπὸ τοῦ τοιούτου ψόγου ἢ ἐπαίνου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 492c; Χρυσίῳ -κεκλυσμένος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 14; -κλυσθέντα πλήθει κακῶν 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 5.1.}}
**II** **wash down** or **away**, κῦμα κ. ψᾶφον ἑλισσομέναν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).10, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.22.3.}}
**II.2** **wash out**, τὰ ἴχνη τοῦ λαγώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.4.}}
**III** **fill full of water**, τὴν πύελον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 843.}}
**IV** **clean out** a bath, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.198.}}

2627:
** κατα-κλυσμός**, ὁ,
**flood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 679d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 222a23, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.337, “Marm.Par.” 6, etc.}}; **inundation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|PMagd. 28v. 4 (3rd c.BC): _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 25c, “Lg.” 677a.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, κ. τῶν πραγμάτων political **deluge**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.214.}}
**II** Medic.. **affusion, douche**, {{Refs|Cael. Aur. “TP” 4.1.1.}}

2629:
** κατα-κόπτω**,
**cut down, fell**, of trees, in _passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.15.1, “CP” 2.15.4, etc.}}
**2** **cut in pieces, cut up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.48, 73, 2.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1688 (_passive_)}}, etc.; κρέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 301c}}; κατακοπείς **cut in pieces**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.92.}}
**3** **cut down, massacre, butcher**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.75, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29}}:—_passive_, ὥσπερ βόες κατεκόπησαν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.235S.}}
**4** in a military sense, **cut in pieces, 'cut up**', τὴν μόραν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.22}}:—_passive_, κατακοπῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.25; κατακεκόψεσθαι [prev. work] 1.5.16.}}
**5** κ. πληγαῖς τινα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 37.20 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**6** generally, **break in pieces, destroy**, στεφάνους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.70; κέραμον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.25.3}}; ἔρια ὑπὸ τῶν σέων κατακοπτόμενα **fretted in pieces**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 730, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 1: _metaphorically_, κ. τὴν ἀρχήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 30; κατακέκοπταί οἱ τὸ τῆς ψυχῆς γαῦρον [prev. author] 2.762f}}; κατεκόπημεν ἄν we **should have been made mince-meat of**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 35.}}
**7** **weary, bore**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 70, 77.}}
**8** Rhet., λέξις -κεκομμένη '**staccato', jerky** composition, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 4.}}
**9** in _middle_, μαστοὺς κατεκόψατο, in vehement grief, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 316 (from Smyrna).}}
**II** **strike with a die, coin** bullion **into money**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.96; τὸν θρόνον ὄντα ἀργυροῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.3; τὰς Χρυσᾶς πλίνθους εἰς νόμισμα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.56, compare 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 281, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 14.45.}}

2630:
** κατακρημν-ίζω**,
_future_ **-ιῶ**{{Refs|“Carmina Popularia” 46.33}}: (κρημνός):—**throw down a precipice**, ἑαυτούς {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.56 W., compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 45, 2.825b, NT.Luke.4.29}}: with a word added, ἀπὸ τοῦ ἄκρου τοῦ κρημνοῦ {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.25.12}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.327, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 1, etc.}}: _perfect_ _participle_, **having fallen over a precipice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.41.}}
**2** generally, **throw headlong down**, ἐκ τριηρέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.31; ἀπὸ τῶν ἵππων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.116.12; ἀπὸ τοῦ πύργου 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.31}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.7.}}

2631:
** κατά-κρῐμα**, ατος, τό,
**condemnation, judgement**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.61.}}
**2** **punishment, fine, damage**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|“CPR” 1.16 (1st c.AD), “PAmh.” 2.114.8 (2nd c.AD), NT.Rom.5.16, 8.1.}}

2632:
** κατα-κρίνω**,
{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **κακρίνω** (see. below) [ῑ], **give as sentence against**, τὸ τελευτῆσαι πάντων ἡ πεπρωμένη κατέκρινε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.43:}} —_passive_, τοῖσι κατεκέκρῐτο θάνατος **sentence** of death **had been passed upon** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.146}}; κατακεκριμένων οἱ τούτων **when** this **sentence has been given against** him, [{{Refs|[prev. author] 2.133; φυγὴν κατακριθείς 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {Ἱεροκλῆς}: _impersonal_, ἂν κατακρῐθῇ μοι}}] if **sentence be given against** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 7: 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, with _dative_}} _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, ὀσέοι ἂν χρηστήριον κακρίνη ἢ γνωσίαι κακριθήη τῶν χρημάτων anyone whom the oracle **has condemned** or who by judicial process **has been condemned** to forfeit his property, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).262.14, 15 (Mantinea, 5th c.BC, = “Class.Phil.” 20.137).}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_, **condemn**, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.2}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, κατέκρινάν μιν ἔκδοτον ἄγεσθαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.85, compare 9.93, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.23 (ubi i.e. βαδίζειν); κ. τινὰ θανάτῳ NT.Matt.20.18, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.13.1; εἴκοσι δραχμαῖς “IG” 5(1).1390.161 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **deem guilty of** a thing, κ. πολλὴν ἄνοιάν τινων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1423b29; ψευδολογίαν τινός 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.14.4}}:—_passive_, **to be condemned**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.54; ψήφῳ θανάτου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 496 codices (_anapaest meter_), compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.18; ἀποθνῄσκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.10}}; also of the crime, τὰ ὁμολογούμενα τῶν πραγμάτων ὑπὸ τοῦ νόμου -κέκριται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.1.1; -κεκρίσθαι τὰ ὑπὸ ἰδιωτῶν πραχθέντα “OGI” 669.27 (Egypt, 1st c.AD).}}
**II** _passive_, simply, **to be judged, deemed**, κατεκρίθη θνατοῖς ἀγανώτατος ἔμμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 149.}}

2633:
** κατά-κρῐσις**, εως, ἡ,
**condemnation**, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.2Cor.3.9, 7.3, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 108.4, 117.35 (_plural_).}}
**2** **judgement**, κ. ψευδής a false **estimate**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.76.}}

2634:
** κατακῡριεύω**,
**gain** or **exercise complete dominion**, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.71(72).8.}}
**2** κ. τινός **gain dominion over, gain possession of**,{{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.9.26(10.5), NT.1Pet.5.3}}; [πλοίου] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.64.}}

2635:
** καταλᾰλ-έω**,
_perfect_ **-λελάληκα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 323.7}}:—**talk, babble loudly**, τοῖς θύραζε ταῦτα κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 752}}; τινος **before** another, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 12.}}
**II** **talk down, rail at**, ἡμᾶς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).338.6 (Thess.); τινὰ πρὸς πάντας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.90.6; τὸ δόγμα [prev. author] 18.45.1}}; τινος {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.44; ὑμῶν ὡς κακοποιῶν NT.1Pet.2.12; τινὸς ψευδῆ LXX.Hos.7.13}}; κατά τινος{{LXX|LXX.Psa.49(50).20}}:—_passive_, ἐπί τινι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.13.2}}; **to be outdone in speech**, ὑπ᾽ ἰδιωτῶν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.343S.}}
**II.2** **weary by talking**, gloss on{καταγλωττίζειν}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.79B.}}
**II.3** simply, **interview, address** a person, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.151 (3rd c.BC).}}

2638:
** καταλαμβάνω**,
_future_ **-λήψομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311a (in _passive_sense, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 48.9)}}, _Ionic dialect_ **-λάμψομαι**{{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.39, _Aeolic dialect_ -λᾱμψομαι uncertain in 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 5.9 (see. λαμβάνω)}}: _perfect_ **-είληφα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250d, etc. (καθ- “SIG” 129.18 (Carpathos, 4th}} c.BC)), **-λελάβηκα** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.122, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42 (variant{-λελαβήκεε})}}]:—_passive_, _Ionic dialect_ _aorist_ **-ελάμφθην**{{Refs|[prev. author] 5.21}}; **-ελάφθην** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 279.7 (Zelea, 4th c.BC)}}: _perfect_ in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.85}}:—**seize, lay hold of**, with _accusative_, τοῦ κατὰ νῶτα λαβών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.433, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 624, etc.; κατέλαβε τὴν ἀκρόπολιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 263 (Lyric poetry, tmesis), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.153, etc. (_metaphorically_, τὴν τοῦ νέου τῆς ψυχῆς ἀκρόπολιν κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 560b)}}; πάντα φυλακαῖς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 33; κ. ἕδρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 21, 86}}; φάσκων Ποσειδῶ πρότερον Ἀθηνᾶς καταλαβεῖν αὐτήν (i.e. τὴν πόλιν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.193}}; later, simply, **arrive at** a place, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1829 (6th c.AD)}}, etc.:—_middle_, **seize for oneself**, τὰ πρήγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.39}}; τὰ ἄλλοι οὐ κατελάβοντο matters which others **had** not **preoccupied**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 55: frequently in 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus, κ. λόφον 1.19.5, +others:}} —_passive_, of a person, ὑπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ καταληφθείς **possessed**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.8.11.}}
**2** of death, fatigue, disaster, etc., τὸν δὲ κατ᾽ ὄσσε ἔλλαβε.. θάνατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.82; Ἄργον.. κατὰ μοῖρ᾽ ἔλαβεν.. θανάτοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.326: with double _accusative_, εὖτ᾽ ἄν μιν κάματος κατὰ γυῖα λάβῃσιν 1.192; Δίκη καταλήψεται ψευδῶν τέκτονας 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 28}}; **befall, overtake**, συμφορὰ κ. πόλεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1161: frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, θεῖα πρήγματα καταλαμβάνει τοὺς αἰελούρους 2.66; πένθεα μεγάλα τοὺς Αἰγυπτίους κ. [prev. passage], compare 3.42}}; ὅσα φεύγοντας ἐκ τῆς πατρίδος κακὰ ἐπίδοξα καταλαμβάνειν may be expected **to befall** them,{{Refs|4.11; ἤν τι καταλαμβάνῃ νεώτερον τὸν πεζόν 8.21}}: followed by _infinitive_, νοῦσός τινα κ. νοσῆσαι {{Refs|3.149, compare 3.75}}; πρίν τι ἀνήκεστον ἡμᾶς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20; κίνδυνος κ. τινά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.99}}; rarely of good fortune, τοῦτον κατέλαβε εὐτυχίη τις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.139.}}
**3** **seize with the mind, comprehend**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370a, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.39, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.2.6, NT.John.1.5}} (perhaps **overcome**); κάλλος διὰ τῆς [ὄψεως] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250d; ἐκ τοῦ φάσματος ὅτι.. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.46, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.5.6}}:—so in _middle_, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.66, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.288; ὅτι.. NT.Act.4.13; τί τὸ πλάτος NT.Eph.3.18}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 22, “Mus.” p.62K., 2nd c.AD: Numenius Apamensis Platonicus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 14.8.}}
**4** **accept**, παρὰ τοῦ βασιλέως.. δωροδοκήματα uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 119.1 (κᾆτ᾽ ἔλαβον Mein.).}}
**II** **catch, overtake, come up with**, τοὺς φεύγοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.63, compare 2.30}}, etc.:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.211, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.47.8.}}
**II.2** **find on arrival**, with _participle_, τινὰ ζῶντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.10; τὰ πλεῖστα.. προειργασμένα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65; πάντα ἔξω [prev. author] 2.18; ἀνεῳγμένην τὴν θύραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174d; τοὺς ἄρχοντας ἐξιόντας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.85; τινὰ ἔνδον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311a; τῶν φορτίων πολλὴν ἀπρασίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.8; τι ὑπάρχον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 131a29}}; **detect**, ἐπ᾽ αὐτοφώρῳ ἐμαυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b}}:—_passive_, κατελήφθη σοῦ λάθρᾳ πωλῶν τὰ σά {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 260, cf. NT.John.8.3, etc.; κατείληπτο σοφιζόμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.164}}; **to be taken by surprise**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 20.}}
**III** _impersonal_, καταλαμβάνει τινά with _infinitive_, **it happens to** one, **it is** one's **fortune** to.., καταλαμβάνει μιν φεύγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.152, compare 3.118; καταλελάβηκέ με.. τοῦτο.. ἐκφῆναι [prev. author] 3.65, compare 4.105, 6.38.}}
**IV** _absolutely_, πρὸς τὴν καταλαβοῦσαν συμφορήν **that had befallen**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.161}}; τὰ καταλαβόντα, ={τὰ συμβάντα}, **what had happened, the circumstances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.49; ἢν πόλεμος καταλάβῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54, compare 18; εἰ -λαμβάνοι ἀναχώρησις [prev. author] 4.31}}; τῆς νυκτὸς -λαμβανούσης as night **was coming on**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.86; Χειμῶνος ἤδη -λαμβάνοντος 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.2.9.}}
**V** **hold down, cover**, τῇ Χειρὶ τὸν ὀφθαλμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 165b; τὰς Χεῖρας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 26}}; **fasten down**, κ. πῶμα γόμφοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.356c, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.358}} (so in _middle_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.37)}}:—_passive_, **to** **be compressed**, opposed to διαλύεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 870b11; τὰς φλέβας -λαμβανόμενοι [prev. author] “Somn.Vig.” 455b7.}}
**V.2** **keep under, repress, check**, κ. τινῶν αὐξανομένην τὴν δύναμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46}}; κ. τὸ πῦρ **get** it **under**,{{Refs|[prev. work] 87; ἴσχε καὶ κ. σεωυτόν [prev. author] 3.36}}; κ. τὰς διαφοράς **put an end to** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.9}}.; κ. ἐρίζοντας **stop** their quarrelling, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.128}}: followed by _infinitive_, κ. τοὺς Αἰγυπτίους ταῦτα μὴ ποιέειν {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.162}}; ὁ τῶν Περσέων θάνατος καταλαμφθεὶς ἐσιγήθη **inquiries about** their death **being checked.. **, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.21.}}
**V.2.b** κ. τὸ πνεῦμα **hold** the breath, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.176, +others}}
**V.3** **bind**, κ. πίστι καὶ ὁρκίοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.106; ὅρκοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86, etc.}}:—_passive_, εἴ τινι -λέλαμμαι ὅρκῳ {{Refs|“SIG” 360.41 (from Chersonesus)}}; νόμοις, ἔθεσι κατειλημμένα **enforced**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1324b22; ζημίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 823a}}; [τὰς σπονδὰς] ηὗρε κατειλημμένας he found the treaty **concluded**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.21 codices}}
**V.4** **compel, constrain** one **to do**, with _infinitive_, ἀναγκαίη μιν κ. φαίνειν **forces** him to bring out the truth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.75:}} —_passive_, ἀναγκαίῃ καταλαμβανόμενος **being constrained**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.65, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.57.}}
**V.5** **convict, condemn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.11}}; opposed to ἀπολύειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.4.9; ἐὰν καταληφθεὶς ἀποθάνω [prev. author] 2.2.9, compare “IG” 12(2).526A20 (Eresus, 4th c.BC)}}; of the prosecutor, **secure a conviction,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Rev.Phil. 1928.192 (Erythrae, 5th c.BC)}}; ὁ -λαβών {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 578.58 (Teos, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}

2639:
** καταλέγω**,
**B** **recount, tell at length and in order**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always in _future_ or _aorist_ 1, ταῦτα μάλ᾽ ἀτρεκέως καταλέξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.413, +others; τόδε εἰπὲ καὶ ἀτρεκέως κατάλεξον [prev. work] 384, +others}}; πᾶσαν ἀληθείην κατάλεξον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|24.407: frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 4.83, 114}}; ἑξῆς κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.610b; κ. τὰς προσηγορίας [prev. work]c:}} —_passive_, τούτων ὦν τῶν καταλεχθέντων of those **which have been recounted**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.50, compare 23, +others}}:—_middle_, Ps.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus “Vitae Homeri” 21}}: followed by _interrogative_ _adverb_, κατάλεξον ὅπως ἤντησας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.44, 3.97}}; κεῖνον ὀϊζυρὸν κατάλεξον, ἤ που ἔτι ζώει.. **tell** me **the tale of** that unhappy man, {{Refs|4.832.}}
**B.b** **repeat, recite**, τῶν Χρησμῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6; τετράμετρα πρὸς τὸν αὐλόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 6.3; τὰς πατρίους εὐχάς 4th c.BC: Hermias Historicus 2}}; καταλέγεσθαι· ὀδύρεσθαι τὸν τεθνεῶτα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; compare κατάλεγμα, καταλογή 111.}}
**B.2** **reckon up, tell in full tale**, μνηστῆρας ἀριθμήσας κατάλεξον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.235}}; of a line of kings or ancestors, κατέλεγον οἱ ἱρέες ἐκ βύβλου.. βασιλέων τ καὶ λ οὐνόματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.100; τοὺς αἰεὶ πατέρας [prev. author] 6.53}}; κ. ἑωυτὸν μητρόθεν **reckoned up** his pedigree, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.173; κ. τοὺς ἄρχοντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 285e, compare “Ep.” 327e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.4}}:—later in _middle_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.187, 2.593, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.504f.}}
**B.2.b** **reckon, count as**, οὒς οἱ πολλοὶ πλουσίους κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 742e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.27}}: so perhaps in _passive_, Χήρα -έσθω {{NT|NT.1Tim.5.9.}}
**B.2.c** **conclude by enumeration**, ὡς.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh. Al.” 1429b35.}}
**B.3** with _perfect_ κατείλοχα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.24.1:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ (see. below): _perfect_ κατείλεγμα; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _pluperfect_ κατειλέχατο {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.1.15}}:— **enumerate, draw up a list**, hence, **enrol, enlist**, ἄνδρας οἳ δορυφόροι μὲν οὐκ ἐγένοντο Πεισιστράτου, κορυνηφόροι δέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59}}; στρατιώτας, ὁπλίτας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1065, “Lys.” 394, etc.; ἱππέας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 49.2; κ. εἰς ὁπλίτας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 15.7; εἰς τὸν κατάλογον Ἀθηναίων [prev. author] 25.16; ἐς τὰς ναῦς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75: generally, τοὺς πεντακισχιλίους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 29.5; κ. τὸν Ἡρακλέα εἰς τοὺς δώδεκα θεούς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.39: with _dative_, κ. τινὰ τοῖς δημοσίᾳ ἱππεύουσιν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.22.3,compare 1.25.3 (nisi to be read ἐγκατ-): with}}_infinitive_, τοὺς πλουσιωτάτους ἱπποτροφεῖν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.15}}:—in _middle_, **enrol for oneself**, δορυφόρους, ὁπλίτας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.98, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.31:}} —_passive_ (_aorist 2_ κατελέγην more common in _Attic dialect_ than _aorist_ 1,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare “IG” 22.896.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762e, 943a)}}, **to be enlisted** or **enrolled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.1}}; τῶν τρισχιλίων κ. **to be enrolled** of their number, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.8; κ. στρατιώτης [prev. author] 9.4; κατειλεγμένος ἱππεύειν [prev. author] 16.13; καταλεγεὶς τῶν τριηράρχων 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.5; ὁ κατειλεγμένος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.8}}; εἰς τὴν σύγκλητον κ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 13}}; ἀγορανόμον -λεγέντα, = Latin _adlectum inter aedilicios, Ann.epigram _{{Refs|1905.120.}}
**B.4** later, **select**, τοῖς παισὶ τοὺς διδασκάλους {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 105:}} —_middle_, τὸν πλωτικὸν [βίον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 368b.}}
**B.II** ={μηνύειν}, τῷ βασιλεῖ τὴν ἐπιβουλήν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.3.2}}: with _genitive_, **inform against**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 19.6.3}}; **accuse**, μάρτυς ἄδικος -λέγων αὐτοῦ ἀσέβειαν {{LXX|LXX.Deu.19.16.}}

2641:
** καταλείπω**,
later **καταλιμπάνω** (which see), _Epic dialect_ also **καλλείπω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.238}}: _future_ καλλείψω {{Refs|14.89}}: _aorist_ κάλλῐπον {{Refs|12.92}}: _aorist 1_ _subjunctive_ καλλείψῃς {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 10.299}}; _participle_ καλλείψας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 32.130; καταλείψας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 7.3}}; _Ionic dialect_ _iterative_ καταλίπεσκε (κατελίπεσκε, καταλειπέεσκε codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.78}}: _perfect_ **-λέλοιπα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 736:}} —_middle_, _future_ καταλείψομαι (in _passive_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.12}}: _aorist 2_ **-ελιπόμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 209d (in _passive_ sense, “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.161 (from Cyrene))}}:—_passive_, _future_ καταλειφθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.7, 17.1}}:—**leave behind**, πὰρ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ὄχεσφιν ἄλλον.. κάλλιπεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.92}}; especially of persons dying or going into a far country, κὰδ δέ με Χήρην λείπεις ἐν μεγάροισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|24.725; οὖρον.. κατέλειπον ἐπὶ κτεάτεσσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.89; οἷόν μιν Τροίηυδε κιὼν κατέλειπεν Ὀδυσσεύς 17.314; so later, τὴν στρατιὴν καταλίπεσκε ἐν τῷ προαστίῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.78; φύλακον κ. τινά [prev. author] 1.113, compare 2.103:}} —_middle_, καταλείπεσθαι παῖδας **leave behind one**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” [same place], compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.34, etc.}}:— _passive_, **to be left, remain behind**, κατελέλειπτο ἐν Πέρσῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.209, compare 7.170, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.12: with _genitive_, [στρατὸς] καταλελειμμένος τοῦ ἄλλου στρατοῦ}} a force **left behind** the rest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.96.}}
**2** **bequeath**, [τόξον] παιδὶ κάλλιπ᾽ ἀποθνῄσκων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.33: _metaphorically_, ἐμοὶ δ᾽ ὀδύνας τε γόους τε κάλλιπεν 1.243, compare 11.279; δόκησιν ἰσχύος καὶ ξυνέσεως ἐς τὸ ἔπειτα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.18; τοῖς θρέψασι λύπας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.70}}; παισὶν αἰδῶ οὐ Χρυσὸν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729b: with}}_infinitive_, εἰ καταλείψει μηδὲ ταφῆναι not enough to be buried with, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 556:}} —_passive_, [Χρήματα] καταλειφθέντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.45.}}
**2.b** κ. διαθήκας **leave** a will (when going on service), {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.14.}}
**3** _middle_, **leave** in a certain state, κόλπον βαθὺν καταλιπόμενος τοῦ κιθῶνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125.}}
**II** **forsake, abandon**, οὕτω δὴ μέμονας Τρώων πόλιν.. καλλείψει; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.89, compare 22.383}}; πολλοὺς καταλείψομεν we **shall leave** many **upon the field**,{{Refs|12.226; ὤ μοι, εἰ μέν κε λίπω κάτα τεύχεα 17.91; κὰδ δέ κεν εὐχωλὴν Πριάμῳ καὶ Τρωσὶ λίποιεν Ἀργείην Ἑλένην 2.160}}: with _infinitive_, κάλλιπεν οἰωνοῖσιν ἕλωρ καὶ κύρμα γενέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.271}}; σχεδίην ἀνέμοισι φέρεσθαι κ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 5.344; μέλη.. θηρσὶν βοράν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 46 (Lyric poetry); μή ποτ᾽ ἐμὸν κατ᾽ αἰῶνα λίποι θεῶν πανάγυρις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 219; μή με καταλίπῃς μόνον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 809; οἰκίας τε καὶ ἱερά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.16}}; πατέρας καὶ ξυγγενεῖς ἀτίμους κ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.58}}; κ. τὴν δίαιταν **not to appear at** the trial, Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.93.}}
**II.2** **let drop, give up**, τὰ αὑτῶν ἔργα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.10, compare 10.15; εἰ ἐνταῦθα -λίποιμι τὸν λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.33.}}
**III** **leave remaining**, ὀκτὼ μόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.5}} codices; κ. ἄφοδον **leave** an exit, {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.2.11}}:—_middle_, κ. στενὴν διέξοδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 73e}}; -λείπεσθαι ἑαυτῷ **reserve** for oneself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.8; ὑπερβολὴν οὐ κ. Χαρᾶς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.23.4, compare 16.25.6}}:—_passive_, **to be left, remain**, τίς ἔτι ἡδονὴ -λείπετα; {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.71, cf. NT.Heb.4.1, etc.}}; of the **remainder** in calculations, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.326, +others (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.13.13, etc.: _impersonal_ καταλείπεται}} with _infinitive_, **it remains that.. **, τὸν κόσμον κ. ἀθάνατον εἶμεν Aristaeusap.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.20.6, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 37.16, etc.}}; -λείπεται μάχη **yet remains to be fought**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.11.}}
**III.2** **leave alone**, opposed to περιαιρέω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 3.2.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1342a34.}}
**III.2.b** **leave undisputed**, τὰς παραλλαγάς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 24}}: hence, **admit, allow the truth of** a doctrine, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Po.” 5.34, 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1055.13}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 80.}}
**III.2.c** **omit**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Sophisticos Elenchos commentarium - pars” 118.10.}}

2643:
** καταλλ-ᾰγή**, ἡ,
**exchange**, especially of money, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1346b24, “PHib.” 1.100.4 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **money-changer's profit, agio**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.30, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 66.14, 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 3.4.}}
**3** **freight, merchandise**, _metaphorically_, [ἀραὶ] βαρεῖαι κ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 767 (Lyric poetry).}}
**4** **change, difference**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.74 K.}}
**II** **change from enmity to friendship, reconciliation**, καταλλαγὰς ποιεῖσθαι πρός τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.4; κ. πολέμου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1588.}}
**II.2** **reconciliation** of sinners **with God**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.5.18; κόσμου NT.Rom.11.15.}}

2644:
** καταλλ-άσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **καταλλάττω**, **change** money, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 18, etc.}} (also in _middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.114}}:—_passive_, Matreas cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.19b}}, with a play on _signification_ {{Refs|11)}}; **change** or **give away**, τὴν Χάριν τῶν νόμων for the laws, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.21 (assuming variant)}}; καταλλάσσειν τὸν βίον **to leave** life, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 5.2.}}
**b** _absolutely_, **transgress, contravene** regulations, {{Refs|“IG” 5(2).3.2 (from Tegea).}}
**2** _middle_, **exchange** one thing **for** another, ἡδονὰς πρὸς ἡδονάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69a}}; ἀντί τινος πάντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. passage],compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Vit.Herc.” 1457.10; βίον πρὸς μικρὰ κέρδη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1117b20; τι ἐπ᾽ ἀργυρίῳ 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.13.6}}: _absolutely_, **exchange prisoners**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 57.36.}}
**II** **change** a person **from enmity to friendship, reconcile**, σφέας {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.29, compare 95, 6.108; κ. τινὰς πρὸς ἀλλήλους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1348b9; θεὸς κόσμον κ. ἑαυτῷ NT.2Cor.5.19:—}}_middle_, καταλλάσσεσθαι τὴν ἔχθρην τινί **to make up one's** enmity with any one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61, compare 7.145:}} —_passive_, especially in _aorist_ κατηλλάχθην or κατηλλάγην (former preferred by Trag., latter in Prose), **to become reconciled**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1157, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.1, etc.; πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59}}; θεοῖσιν ὡς καταλλαχθῇ Χόλου that **he may be reconciled** to them after his anger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 744; κ. πρός τινα ἐκ διαφορᾶς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.21.}}
**II.2** _passive_, of an offence, **to be atoned for**, φόνον ἐπιγαμίαις μὴ καταλλάσσεσθαι μηδὲ Χρήμασιν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 218.105 (Ilium, 3rd c.BC).}}

2647:
** κατα-λύω**,
_future_ **-λύσω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.28}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _pluperfect_ **-λελύκεσαν**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.4.2}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-λῠθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 714c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.22 (_future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, see below 1.2a)}}: _perfect_ **-λέλῠμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.36}}:— **put down, destroy**, πολλάων πολίων κατέλυσε κάρηνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.117, 9.24}}; τείχη, [πτόλιν], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 819, 1080 (both Lyric poetry)}}; γέφυραν **break** it **up**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus [prev. cited]}}
**2** of political or other systems, **dissolve, break up, put down**, κ. ἀρχήν, βασιληΐην, ἰσοκρατίας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53, 54, 5.92. ; τοῦ Διὸς τὴν δύναμιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 142; τὸ κράτος τῆς βουλῆς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 7; τὰς προσόδους τὰς Μιλησίων “SIG” 633.40 (Milet., 2nd c.BC)}}: frequently in _Attic dialect_, κ. τὸν δῆμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 453, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.81; τὴν δημοκρατίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 948}}; τὰς πολιτείας Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.182}}:—_passive_, καταλελυμένης τῆς δημοκρατίας Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.96, compare 95, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292a29}}: _future_ _middle_ as _passive_, καταλύσεται.. ἡ ἀρχή (Cobet καταλελύσεται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.9.}}
**2.b** with _accusative_ _person_, **put down, depose**, κ. τύραννον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18, etc.; κ. τινὰ τῆς ἀρχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.24:}} — _passive_, τῶν ἄλλων καταλελυμένων στρατηγῶν **having been dismissed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.43; καταλυθῆναι τῆς ἀρχῆς [prev. author] 1.104, compare 6.9.}}
**2.c** **dissolve, dismiss, disband** a body, καταλύειν τὴν βουλήν, τὸν στόλον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.72, 7.16. ; τῶν πόλεων τά τε βουλευτήρια καὶ τὰς ἀρχάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15; τὸ ναυτικόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.102 (_passive_).}}
**2.d** **abolish** or **annul** laws, customs, etc., δίκην {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Pal.” 17; νόμους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.66 (_passive_), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.8.2, cf. NT.Matt.5.17; ψήφισμα “Michel” 725.20 (2nd c.BC)}}; also κ. τὸν ἱππέα **render** him **useless**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 12.5.}}
**2.e** τὴν φυλακὴν κ. **neglect** the watch, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 2, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308a29; τὴν φρουράν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762c; τὴν κοινὴν φυλακὴν καταλυθῆναι βούλεται 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.112.}}
**2.f** κ. τὴν τριηραρχίαν **lay** it **down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.59}}; τὴν ἄσκησιν, see below 3a.
**3** **bring to an end**, τὸν βίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 7; ἐς Ἅιδαν καταλύσουσ᾽ ἔμμοχθον βίοτον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1004 (Lyric poetry); μώμου ἀδικίαν καὶ δόξης ἀμαθίαν 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Hel.” 21; ἐλπίδα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89; δόξα, ἣν αἰσχρόν ἐστιν ἐν σοὶ -λῦσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.73}}; κ. τὸ πλεῖν, τὴν ἄροσιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 33.4, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 13.1; κ. τὰς θυσίας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.68; τὰ γυμνάσια 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.39; τὸν λόγον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.176}}; τοὺς λόγους περὶ τὰ μέγιστα κ. {{Refs|[prev. work]199: _absolutely_}}**make an end**, ὥρᾳ κ. **die** in good time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Diocles Comicus 14, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.28}}; πύκτης ὢν κατέλυσε **retired** from the ring, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.79 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), compare 161 ([prev. author]) (in full -λῦσαι τὴν ἄσκησιν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “προτρεπτικός - Scripta Minora” 14)}}; καθάπερ ἐν τοῖς Χοροῖς ἐν τῷ καταλύειν in the **ending**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 921a20: also}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ καταλελυμένος **disused, obsolete**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.68 K.}}
**3.b** κ. τὴν ὑπάρχουσαν εἰρήνην **break** the peace, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.55}}; but,
**3.c** more commonly, κ. τὸν πόλεμον **end** the war, make peace, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 112, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.27, etc.}}; δίκας **settle** disputes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).357.15 (Stymphalus, 3rd c.BC): _absolutely_ (i.e. τὸν πόλεμον), Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23; πρός τινα Foed.[prev. work] 8.58:}} —more frequently in _middle_, καταλύσασθαι τὰς ἔχθρας, **componere inimicitias**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.146; τὸν πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.17, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.36; στάσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 359}}: _absolutely_, **make peace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.8.6, etc.}}; καταλύεσθαί τινι **come to terms with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.11, etc.}}
**4** _passive_, ἤδη καταλελυμένης τῆς ἡλικίας in the **decay** of life, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335a34.}}
**II** **unloose, unyoke**, καταλύσομεν ἵππους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.28}}; τὸ σῶμα τοῦ ἀδελφεοῦ κ. **take** it **down** from the wall where it was hung up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.γ}}:—_passive_, **to be taken down** from hanging, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.43.}}
**II.2** _intransitive_, **take up one's quarters, lodge**, παρ᾽ ἐμοὶ καταλύει he **is** my **guest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447b, compare “Prt.” 311a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.82: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315d: with _accusative_, κ. παρά τινα}} **turn off** the road to a person's house, **go and lodge** with him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.136; κ. εἰς πανδοκεῖον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.97; Μεγαροῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142c; ἐν τῷ ἱαρῷ “SIG” 978.8 (Cnidos, 3rd c.BC):}} —_middle_, θανάτῳ καταλυσαίμαν **may I take my rest** in the grave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 146 (_anapaest meter_).}}

2648:
** καταμανθάνω**,
_perfect_ **-μεμάθηκα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 8, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.1}}:—**observe well, examine closely**, τὴν στρατιήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.146; τὸν Οἰνέα 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 6.16, compare 10; τὸ τραῦμα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 34}}; **look to, inspect**, τὸν ἐλαιῶνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PFay.” 114.11 (100 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus); κ. ἤν που.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.3.}}
**2** **learn, acquire knowledge of**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 198d, etc.}}; ὑπακούειν how to obey, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 13.7.}}
**3** **perceive with the senses, observe**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 960a7}}: more commonly with the mind, **understand, perceive, observe**, οὐκ ὀρθῶς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 128a; εἰ ἄρα μου καταμανθάνετε ὃ λέγω [prev. author] “Lg.” 689c; ἐκ τῶν νόμων κ. τοὺς λόγους εἰ ὀρθῶς.. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14; κ. ὁπόσα θνητῇ φύσει δυνατά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 986c}}; κ. ὅτι.. Hp.[same place]; ῥᾴδιον τοῦτο κ., ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285a1}}: _perfect_, **to be aware**, Λυκοῦργον -μεμάθηκας ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.15}}: with _accusative_ et _participle_, κ. πολλοὺς ἔχοντάς τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.1.1; καταμαθόντες μιν ἀγοράζοντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.164; κ. τινὰ θύοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.2}}; καταμαθὼν δὲ.. καταστασιαζόμενος that a party was being formed against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 1.6.4: καταμαθεῖν τοῦ Κύρου δοκοῦμεν, ὡς.. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.1.40.}}
**4** **consider**, τι {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.1.44; ὅτι.. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.5.80, etc.}}

2649:
** καταμαρτῠρέω**,
**bear witness against**, τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.120, 29.9, Mitteis “Chr.” 31v33 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; κατά τινος {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.3, etc.: with _accusative_ _of things_, ψευδῆ κ. τινός [prev. author] 45.46 (Docum.), 29.2, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.12, cf. NT.Matt.26.62: _absolutely_, αὐτὸ τὸ ψήφισμα τῆς βουλῆς—μαρτυρήσει 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.28}}:—_passive_, **have evidence given against** one, μὴ πιστῶς καταμαρτυρηθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.7}}; κ. ὑπὸ τοῦ βίου τοῦ ἑαυτοῦ **to be convicted**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.90.}}
**2** _passive_, of evidence, **to be given against** one, ἃ καταμαρτυρεῖται αὐτοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.25, compare 6.15: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.55.}}
**II** **assert concerning**, οὐδὲν κ. τῶν οὐ παρόντων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.13.}}
**III** Astrology texts, **exercise malign influence over, 'aspect**', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 104.2.}}

2650:
** καταμένω**,
_future_ **-μενῶ**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 197}}:—**stay**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1373, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.103, 121.δ}}, etc.; ἐνθάδ᾽ αὐτοῦ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1187; ἐνταῦθα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.17; κ. ἐν τοῖς δήμοις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.18; παρά τινι 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 21}}; **reside,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|PHal. 1.183 (3rd c.BC), NT.Act.1.13; ἐν ἐποικίῳ “PFay.” 24 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **remain fixed, continue** in a state, ἐν τοῖς ὑπηρετικοῖς ὅπλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.18; ἐπὶ τῶν αὐτῶν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.328; ἔν τινι [prev. author] 2.27; ἐπὶ τοῖς ὑπάρχουσι Nymphodorus Historicus 21: _absolutely_, τῆς εἰωθυίας ἀρχῆς καταμενούσης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.30.}}

2654:
** κατανᾱλ-ίσκω**,
_imperfect_ **-ανάλισκον** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 1.18}}]: _pluperfect_ -ανηλώκει (_intransitive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ti.” 36b}}: but _aorist_ **-ηνάλωσα** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 9.60}}]:—_passive_, _aorist_ **-αναλωθῆναι** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Phaedo” 72d}}]; _subjunctive_ **-αναλωθῇ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.4.1}}; _indicative_ -ηναλώθησαν {{Refs|[prev. work] 2}}: _perfect_ **-ανήλωμαι** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 3.31}}] codices; _infinitive_ [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|κατηναλῶσθαι Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] 2.112a}}]:— **use up, spend, lavish**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Χρήματα Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 1.2.22}}]; εἴς τι upon a thing, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|εἰς τὴν στρατείαν τάλαντα μύρια Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 9.60; τὴν σχολὴν εἰς φιληκοΐαν [prev\. author] 1.18; τὰς δυνάμεις εἰς τὰ ἄλογα Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Protagoras” 321c; τέσσαρας μνᾶς εἰς ὀψοφαγίαν 3rd c.BC: Ister Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.345d}}]; of space in a treatise, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1508.10; also κ. πολλὰ ἡδοναῖς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.108; τὸ πλεῖστον τοῦ βίου ἐν ὁμιλίᾳ Aelianus [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Varia Historia” 3.13}}]:—_passive_, with _pluperfect_ _active_, **to be lavished**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ti.” [same place]; εἴς τι [prev\. author]“Phd.”[same place]; πάσας [τιμὰς] κατηναλῶσθαι ἄλλοις Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}]}}] [prev. cited]
**2** **consume**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|τὴν τροφήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 763a13, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus}} 2.160b; **devour** fuel, of fire, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Juv.” 469b29}}; later, **eat**, [ἰχθύν] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 109; [ῥοιᾶς κόκκον] 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.5.3:}} —_passive_, ἡ τροφὴ κ. εἰς τὴν αὔξησιν, εἰς τὸ σῶμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “GA” 771a28, 725b31, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “VM” 11; ἐπιστήμη οὐ κ. ὑπὸ πόνων Andronic.Rhod. p.578 M.}}

2655:
** καταναρκάομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **καταναρκ-έομαι**, _passive_, **grow quite numb**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 48}}; **to be reduced to a torpid condition**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Lib.” p.52 O.}}
**II** _active_, καταναρκᾶν τινος **to be slothful towards, press heavily upon.. **, {{NT|NT.2Cor.11.9, 12.13.}}

2657:
** κατανο-έω**,
**observe well, understand, apprehend**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ὡς ἐμὲ κατανοέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28, compare 93; οὐ Χαλεπὸν τῷ βουλομένῳ κ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.34; οὐ.. κατανοῶ τὸ νῦν ἐρωτώμενον Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Sophista” 233a; κ. ὅ τι λέγω [prev\. author] “Grg.” 455b; οὐ πάνυ κατανοῶ [prev\. author] “Phlb.” 48a; κ. ὅτι.. [prev\. author] “Sph.” 264b}}]; κατανοεῖς τίς ποτ᾽ ἐστίν..; [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 33.1; ἐκεῖνο, ὅτι.. Epicurus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “Epistulae” 1p.30U.; ἐκ τίνων.. καὶ πότε.. καὶ πῶς.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.12.9}}]:—_passive_, of a doctrine, **to be grasped and** hence **accepted**, μᾶλλον μᾶλλον κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Nat.” 138G.; εἰς καρδίαν -εῖται}} **is understood** of.., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.1.}}
**2** **perceive**, τῷ κατανοουμένῳ τὸ κατανοοῦν ἐξομοιῶσαι **the percipient to the perceived**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ti.” 90d}}, etc.: with _participle_, κ. οὐ πολλοὺς ὄντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3.}}
**3** **learn**, {{Refs|τῆς Περσίδος γλώσσης ὅσα ἐδύνατο κατενόησε [prev\. author] 1.138}}.
**4** **consider**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|περί τινος Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.20}}].
**5** **look at, view**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τὴν οἰκίαν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.179a}}.
**II** **to be in one's right mind, in one's senses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Epid.” 1.26.γ, 5.39.}}

2658:
** καταντ-άω**,
**come down to, arrive**, εἰς τὰ βασίλεια, ἐπὶ κοίτην, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.52, 3.27, compare “PTeb.” 59.3 (1st c.BC), etc.: _metaphorically_, ἐπὶ τὴν φυσικὴν ὁδόν 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 259.3, compare 185.16, 251.30.}}
**2** in a speech or narrative, **come to, arrive at** a point, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|εἰς τὴν ἔκπτωσιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.1.8; ἐπί τινας λογισμούς [prev\. author] 10.37.3; κ. ἐπὶ τὸν ὅρκον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.79, cf. J “AJ” 3.10.4, etc.}}; **have recourse to**, ἐπὶ [τὴν ἡδονήν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Ep.” 3p.63U.; ἐπὶ τὰ δάκρυα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.62}} O.
**3** of persons, κ. εἰς ἑαυτούς **attack, commence hostilities against** each other, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.11.3.}}
**4** of events, **come upon**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|πᾶς δ᾽ ἀγὼν ἐπ᾽ ἐμὲ κατήντα 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.13}}; κ. εἴς τινα **affect** him, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p}}.83 W.; of blood-guiltiness, **fall**, {{Refs|ἐπὶ κεφαλήν τινος Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum “2 Kings” 3.29}}.
**4.b** **turn out, result**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|ποῦ καταντήσει πάλιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.4.12; τὸ πρᾶγμα κ. εἰς ὑπόνοιαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.37; εἰς τὸ μηδέν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.34.2}}; so of numbers, **to be reduced**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|εἰς μόνους ἄνδρας δέκα “BGU” 903.14 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**5** of an inheritance, κ. εἴς τινα **fall** to one's share, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|“NT.1Cor.10.11”, “POxy.” 75 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** _transitive_, **make to come back, bring back**, τινα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 2; εἰς ἑαυτὸν τὴν ἀρχιερωσύνην Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum “2 Maccabees” 4.24}} (so _intransitive_ in _perfect_, **return**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|εἰς τὸν αὐτὸν κατηντηκέναι βίον “BGU” 1101.5 (1st c.BC)).}}

2659:
** κατά-νυξις**, εως, ἡ,
**stupefaction, bewilderment**, {{LXX|ἐπότισας ἡμᾶς οἶνον -νύξεως Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum “Psalms” 59(60).3, cf. LXX.Isa.29.10.}}
**2** **contrition**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Nov.” 137.6 “Intr.”}}

2661:
** καταξι-όω**,
**deem worthy**, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.111}}; **hold in honour**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.86.8:}} with _genitive_ _of things_, **deem worthy of** a thing, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|τάξεως τὸν κίνδυνον [prev\. author] 1.23.3; τινὰ μεγάλης ἀποδοχῆς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.60}}:—_middle_, οὔτε νιν.. Δίκη προσεῖδε καὶ κατηξιώσατο did not regard and **hold in high esteem**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Th.” 667:}} —_passive_, **to be held worthy**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|πρεσβείας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.10.8, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “VP” 36.265; ἔργον ἐπιφανὲς καὶ κατηξιωμένον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.83.4}}.
**II** **command, bid**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|πολλὰ Χαίρειν ξυμφοραῖς καταξιῶ Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Agamemnon” 572}}]; σύ τοι κατηξίωσας thou **didst decree it so**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” 1095 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **deign, vouchsafe**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 3, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Ep.” 204, “PLond.” 2.232.14 (4th c.AD), “POxy.” 1214 (5th c.AD), etc.}}
**IV** in bad sense, τῶν ἐν μέρους εἴδει πεφυκότων μηδενὶ καταξιώσωμεν **let us** not **degrade it by likening it to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ti.” 30c.}}
**V** in argument, **claim, maintain**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Sign.” 30, +others:}} —_middle_, with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Rh.” 1.32S.}}

2664:
** κατα-παύω**,
_poetry_ **καππαύω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.15, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “Scol.Oxy.” 1.2}}:— **put an end to, stop**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|κατέπαυσα θεῶν Χόλον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.583}}; μηνιθμὸν καταπαυσέμεν (_Epic dialect_ _future_ _infinitive_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.62; πόλεμον καταπαυσέμεν ἀνδρῶν 7.36}}; νεῖκος κ. [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Th.” 87; τὴν ναυπηγίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27; νόσους Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Supplices” 586}}] (Lyric poetry); λιγυρὰν γᾶρυν B.[same place]; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|αἱμορραγίαν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.777}}; **bring to a close**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|τὸν λόγον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.8.8; τὸ σύγγραμμα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.26}}; κ. τὸν πρῶτον λόγον εἰς.. **conclude** the first section **and proceed** to.., {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Mete.” 78.9:}} — _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|πόνους -παυόμενοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1154}} (Lyric poetry):—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|-παύεται τὰ ἀρρωστήματα τοῖς τῶν ἰατρῶν εὑρήμασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.26}}.
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **put an end to**, i.e. **kill**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|τάχα κέν σε.. ἔγχος ἐμὸν κατέπαυσε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.618; σοῦ κ. τὰς πνοάς Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 1397}}].
**II.2** **make one stop from** a thing, **hinder, check**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|μιν καταπαύσῃ ἀγηνορίης ἀλεγεινῆς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.457; παῖδας καταπαυέμεν ἀφροσυνάων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.457; so κ. τινὰ δρόμου Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Politicus” 294e}}]: with _participle_, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|κ. ταύτην λαλοῦσαν Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 66.5}}]: with _accusative_ only, **keep in check**, τινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.244 (compare 168), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.105.}}
**II.3** **depose from** power, κ. τινὰ τῆς ἀρχῆς, τῆς βασιληΐης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.1, 6.64; τοὺς τυράννους [prev\. author] 5.38, compare 2.144, 7.105; Μούσας}} **depose** them **from their honours, cease to worship** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “HF” 685 (Lyric poetry):}} —_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τῆς βασιληΐης κατεπαύσθη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.130, compare 6.71.}}
**II.3.b** **put down**, {{Refs|τὴν ἑωυτοῦ ἀρχήν [prev\. author] 1.86}}; τὴν Κύρου δύναμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 90; δῆμον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107; τοὺς τετρακοσίους [prev\. author] 8.97; τιμὰς ἐνέρων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 31}} (_anapaest meter_).
**III** _passive_ and _middle_ (_future_ {{Refs|-πᾰήσομαι “PMag.Lond.” 121.916)}}, **leave off, cease**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.” 1265; λόγος κ. ἐν.. Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Philebus” 66c}}]: with _participle_, οὐ -παήσεται ἐρχομένη {{Refs|“PMag.” [prev. cited]}}
**IV** _active_ used _intransitive_ like _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|μολπᾶν δ᾽ ἄπο.. καταπαύσας πόσις.. ἔκειτο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 918}} (Lyric poetry, assuming variant); εὐημερῶν κατάπαυσον **rest** while you are well off, {{LXX|“Comedy texts Adesp.” 110.8, cf. LXX.Gen.8.22, +others; κ. τοῦ πορευθῆναι LXX.1Ki.12.24.}}

2665:
** καταπέτασμα**, ατος, τό,
**curtain, veil**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.28, “PGrenf.” 2.111.7 (5th-6th c.AD)}}; especially **the veil of the Temple**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.26.31, Aristeas 86, “NT.Mat.27.51”}}, etc.; properly **the inner veil**, the outer being τὸ κάλυμμα, compare {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.148: _metaphorically_, κ. δόξης [prev\. author] 1.270}}.
**2** κ. τραπέζης table-**cover,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Michel 832.25 (Samos, 4th c.BC).}}

2666:
** καταπίνω** [ῑ],
_future_ **-πίομαι** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Equites” 693}}], later **-πιοῖμαι** [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Alcibiades” 15}}]: _aorist_ [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|κατέπιον “IG” 4.951.102 (Epid.); poetry κάππιον {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” p.45}}] R.: _perfect_ **-πέπωκα** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 1137}}]:—**gulp, swallow down**, both of liquids and solids [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(οὐδ᾽ ἐν τῷ καταπίνειν ἦν πάντως τὸ πίνειν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.478), τοὺς μὲν κατέπινε Κρόνος (i.e. υἱούς) 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Th.” 459, compare 467, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyc.” 219; ὁ τροχίλος.. καταπίνει τὰς βδέλλας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.68, compare 70; τεμάχη Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Nubes” 338}}]; λίθους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author]“Av.” [same place]; [κίχλας] 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.24; [μάζας] 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 1.5}}; of the sea, {{LXX+6th c.BC+|μὴ ναῦν κατὰ κῦμα πίῃ 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 680, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pr.” 931b39 (_passive_); τὸ στόμα [τῆς γῆς] -πίεται αὐτούς Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Num.16.30}}:—_passive_, τὸ -ποθὲν ὕδωρ (i.e. by the earth) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Criti.” 111d}}; of rivers **that disappear underground**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mete.” 351a1; ὑφ᾽ ἅμμου 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.32}}; of cities **swallowed** by an earthquake, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.17; πόλις καταποθεῖσα ὑπὸ τῆς θαλάττης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.41.7}}.
**2** _absolutely_, **swallow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|μόλις καταπίνειν δύνηται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.35, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Nat.Fac.” 3.6.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, τὸν ἡμίοπον ὁ μέγας [αὐλὸς] κ. [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “fragment” 91; καταπιοῦνται ὑμᾶς οἱ Ἀθηναῖοι Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Alcibiades” 15}}]:—_passive_, **to be absorbed**, of knots in wood, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 5.2.2; τῆς -πεπομένης ὑπ᾽ αὐτοῦ φύσεως Damascius Philosophus [{5-6th c.AD}] “de Principiis” 10}}].
**II.b** κ. Εὐριπίδην **drink in** Euripides, i.e. **imbibe** his spirit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ach.” 484, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 1:}} —_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|τὸ τεχνίον ἀεὶ τοῦτό μοι κατεπίνετο 4th c.BC: Antidotus Comicus 2.4}}.
**II.c** **swallow, absorb**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|τὰς τέχνας 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.257}} (_passive_); but, **swallow** one's anger, {{Refs|[prev. work] 242.}}
**II.2** **swallow up, consume**, [the robe] [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐρίων τάλαντον καταπέπωκε ῥᾳδίως Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Vespae” 1147}}]; ὁ δικαστὴς αὐτὰ [the revenue] {{Refs|καταπίνει μόνος [prev\. author] “Ra.” 1466}}; τὸν ναύκληρον αὐτῷ σκάφει κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.19}}; τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Epit.” 151.}}
**II.3** **spend, waste in tippling**, [τὴν οὐσίαν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|οὐ μόνον κατέφαγεν, ἀλλὰ.. καὶ κατέπιεν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.96, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 45.28.}}

2667:
** καταπίπτω**,
_future_ -πεσοῦμαι: _aorist_ κατέπεσον, _poetry_ κάππεσον (the only tense used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), _Doric dialect_ κάπετον (which see), also κατέπετον “IG” 4.951.80}}(Epid.); late 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _singular_ _optative_ **-πέσειεν** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 168.5}} (variant{-οιεν}): _perfect_ -πέπτωκα:—**fall, drop**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|καππεσέτην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.560; κάππεσον ἐν Λήμνῳ 1.593; κάππεσον ἐν κονίῃσι 12.23; πρηνὴς ἐπὶ γαίῃ κάππεσε 16.311, 414; πρηνὴς ἁλὶ κάππεσε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.374; ἀφ᾽ ὑψηλοῦ πύργου 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.386; ἀπὸ τῶν ἡμιπλινθίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50; ἀπὸ τῆς κλίμακος Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 840; ἀπ᾽ ὄνου [prev\. author] “Nu.” 1273; ἀφ᾽ ἵππου Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Oeconomicus” 1.8; ἐς μέσους τοὺς ἄνθρακας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 671; ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.54}}]; πληγεὶς κ. [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.27; οἰκίαι καταπεπτωκυῖαι Andocides Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 1.108, “BGU” 282.7 (2nd c.AD), etc.: used as}}]_passive_ of καταβάλλω, πρὸς ἡμῶν κάππεσε, ={κατεβλήθη}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” 1553 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, παραὶ ποσὶ κάππεσε θυμός their spirit **fell**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.280; μήτε καταπεσὼν ὀδύρεο 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 66.5; πρὸς τὴν φήμην τῆς ἐφόδου -πεσόντες 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 7.4.2, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.20.1; κ. τὴν ψυχήν variant in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “AJ” 6.14.2}}: frequently in _perfect_ _participle_ καταπεπτωκώς, **base, contemptible**, λόγος Aristeas{{Refs 1st c.AD+|144; γένος ἄτιμον καὶ κ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phoc.” 4; ἀγεννεῖς καὶ καταπεπτωκότες 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Declamationes” 30.45; ταῖς ψυχαῖς καὶ τοῖς σώμασι 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 10.136b}}.
**2.b** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|κ. εἰς ἀπιστίαν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Phaedo” 88d; εἰς ἀπορίαν [prev\. author] “Men.” 84c; πρὸς τὸ Χεῖρον 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.16.1}}].
**3** τὰ -πίπτοντα the **accidents** of fortune, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 40.15.}}
**4** τὰς νυνὶ -πεπτωκυίας [ἐμβολάς] which **have** just **been rejected**, Hegetorap.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Apollonius Citiensis Medicus 3.}}
**5** ἄλλα, ἃ -πέπτωκε τούτοις which **fall under the same head**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.723.}}
**II** **have the falling sickness**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 24, “Philops.” 16.}}

2668:
** καταπλέω**,
Ionic καταπλώω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93}} codices: _aorist_ **-έπλωσα** {{Refs|[prev\. author] 1.2}}, +others:— **sail down**; i.e.,
**1** **sail from the high sea to land, put in**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἔνθα κατεπλέομεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.142}}: _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.97, 7.137, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.5, etc.; ἐς Αἶαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, compare 8.132; ἐπὶ Ἑλλησπόντου [prev. work] 109, 9.98; ἐπ᾽ Ἀρτεμίσιον [prev\. author] 7.195}}; τὰς ἐκ Πόντου ναῦς Ἀθήναζε κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 5.1.28; ἕως ἂν δεῦρο -πλεύσωμεν Test. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.168; ἐνταῦθα κ. [prev\. author] 32.14}}; **sail home**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.3, 3rd c.BC: Phoenicides Comicus 2.3; νεωστὶ καταπεπλευκώς}} **having** lately **come ashore**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthd.” 297c}}; of things, **to be brought by sea**, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|πυρὸς Ἀθήναζε -πλέων Theophrastus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “de Causis Plantarum” 4.9.5; ἡ -πλέουσα ἀγορά 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 100}}].
**2** **sail down stream**, [ἐς] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὸν Εὐφρήτην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185}}; in Egypt, **down the Nile**, κ. εἰς τὴν πόλιν (i.e. Alexandria) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMagd.” 22.4 (3rd c.BC), compare “PTeb.” 58.44 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; of fish, **swim down stream**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κ. ἐς θάλασσαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 598b16.}}
**II** **sail back**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165, 3.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.13, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Acad.Ind.” p.102M., etc.}}

2669:
** καταπον-έω**,
**subdue**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|τῇ ἐνδείᾳ τῆς τροφῆς τὴν ἀλκὴν τοῦ θηρίου 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.37, compare 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus “Incred.” 11}}: in _future_ _middle_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|τὰς ὀλίγας ναῦς ταῖς πολλαπλασίαις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.15}}; **worst** in a lawsuit, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1101.9 (4th c.AD):}} —_passive_, **to be subdued, reduced, worn out**, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|δῆμος -πεπονημένος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.36, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.27.11, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.6; πάντα ταῖς ἐνδελεχείαις -πονεῖται πράγματα Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 744}}]; **to be exhausted**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τῷ θάλπει 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.715}}.
**2** **handle roughly, crush, damage**, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|τὰ -πονούμενα καὶ συμπατούμενα Theophrastus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “Historia Plantarum” 8.7.5}}]; **maltreat, oppress**, especially in _passive_, ὑπὸ τῶν τυράννων, ὑπὸ τῶν τελωνῶν, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “fragment” 575, “BGU” 1188.17 (Aug.), cf. NT.Acts.7.24, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 1.}}
**3** **digest** food, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.32 (_passive_).}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _perfect_ _participle_ -πεπονηκώς **ruinous**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Aed.” 1.4, 8.}}

2673:
** καταργ-έω**,
**leave unemployed** or **idle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Χέρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 753}}; κατηργηκέναι τοὺς καιρούς **to have missed** the opportunities, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus “fragment” 176; κ. τὴν γῆν}} **make** the ground **useless, cumber** it, {{NT|“NT.Luke.13.7”.}}
**2** **cause to be idle, hinder** in one's work, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.2Esd.4.21, “POxy.” 38.17 (1st c.AD):—}}_passive_, {{LXX|LXX.2Esd.6.8}}; **to be rendered** or **lie idle,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PFlor. 176.7 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **make of no effect,** NT.Rom.3.3,31, +others:—_passive_, **to be abolished, cease**, {{NT|[prev. work]6.6, NT.1Cor.2.6, etc.; κ. ἀπὸ τοῦ νόμου}} **to be set free** from.., {{NT|NT.Rom.7.2}}; **to be parted**, {{Refs|ἀπὸ Χριστοῦ “Epistle to the Galatians” 5.4}}.

2674:
** κατᾰριθμ-έω**,
**count** or **reckon among**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|μετά τινων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 872}} (_passive_); [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἔν τισι Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Politicus” 266a, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.85, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sol.” 12; εἰς εὐδαιμονίαν κ}}]. **reckon** as.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.9d: with double _accusative_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sph.” 266e:—}}_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pol.” 1329a27; μετά τινων [prev. work] 1293b26; ἔν τισι “Act.Ap.” 1.17, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.239 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**2** **recount in detail**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τὴν ἀτοπίαν σου Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 215a}}]:—_middle_, **recount, enumerate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Phlb.” 27b, “Grg.” 451e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.11; τι πρός τινα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.54}}: _perfect_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, τὰς τῶν πολλῶν κατηριθμημένοι δόξας **having summed up.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Top.” 101a31:}} —_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.78 W.; τὰ συμβεβηκότα -ηρίθμηται 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.281}}.
**3** _middle_, **count** or **reckon** so and so, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|εὐδαιμονέστατον κ. τινά Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Philebus” 47b; ἐν ἀδικήματι κ. τὴν πρᾶξιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.67.5}}].
**II** _absolutely_, **count, reckon**, διὰ τί πάντες ἄνθρωποι εἰς τὰ δέκα κ.; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pr.” 910b24.}}

2676:
** κατάρτ-ῐσις**, εως, ἡ,
**restoration**, {{NT|“NT.2Cor.13.9”.}}
**II** **training, discipline**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alex.” 7.}}
**III** ={καταρτισμός} {{Refs 7th c.AD+|11, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.99.}}

2677:
** καταρτ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**restoration, reconciliation**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX “LXX.Isa.38.12. “}}
**II** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Settling” of a limb, 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.1.1 (_plural_), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.73 (_plural_).}}
**III** **furnishing, preparation**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|αὐλῆς “PTeb.” 33.12 (2nd c.BC); ἱματίον “PRyl.” 127.28 (1st c.AD).}}
**IV** **training, discipline**, {{Refs|τῶν ἁγίων “Epistle to the Ephesians” 4.12}}.

2678:
** κατα-σείω**,
_future_ **-σείσω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Art.” 43}}: _perfect_ **-σέσεικα** [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Philemo Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 84}}]:—**shake down, throw down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|οἰκοδομήματος ἐπὶ μέγα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76}}; τεῖχος, τοῦ τείχους ἐπὶ μέγα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “An.” 1.19.2, 2.23.1; σεισμὸς κ. τὴν πόλιν Ae|. “VH” 6.7:}} —_passive_, **fall down**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.512}}; of a lion's mane, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|αὐχένος ἐκ λασίοιο Χαίτη -εσείετο 2nd c.AD: Pancrates Epicus “POxy.” 1085.21}}: _metaphorically_, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|κ. ἀκροατοῦ ὦτα Philostratus Sophista [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.29; νόμους 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 27.33; κατέσεισεν ἅπαντα καὶ κατεβρόντησε Eunapius Historicus [{4-5th c.AD}] “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.256 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ἕως κατέσεισε}}] until he **laid** him **on the floor** (with drinking), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 8, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus [same place], 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.431c.}}
**2** **impel, drive headlong**, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|νεανίσκον εἰς τὸν μανιώδη καὶ σφαλερὸν τῆς βασιλείας ἔρωτα Eunapius Historicus [{4-5th c.AD}] “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.235 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare p.267 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator:}}] —_passive_, πρὸς τὸ λέγειν κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] p.223 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**3** in Surgery, **treat by shaking**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 42:}} —_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. work] 43.}}
**4** κατασείσας τὴν χεῖρα **with a motion** of the hand, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Acts.19.33; κ. ἱκετηρίας 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.21.8}}; κ. τὰ ἱμάτια, by way of signal, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pomp.” 73: more frequently with _dative_, κ. τῇ Χειρί}} **beckon** with the hand, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.78.3, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.7; κ. τῇ Χειρὶ σιγᾶν “Act.Ap.” 12.17; κ. τῷ λύχνῳ ἅμα λέγων τὸν λόγον “PMag.Lond.” 46.453; κ. ὀθόναις 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.6}}: _absolutely_, κ. τινί **beckon** to another, as a sign for him to be silent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 5.4.4; κ. τισὶν ἐπεξιέναι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.10.2}}; but also, **shake the head** in token of contempt, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Vit.” p.37 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}

2680:
** κατασκευ-άζω**,
_future_ -σκευάσω, _Attic dialect_ **-σκευῶ** {{Refs|“SIG” 1097.9}} (Athens, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC), “IPE” 12.32 B 53 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC): _Doric dialect_}} _aorist_ -εσκεύαξα Ti. _Locrian dialect_94d, {{Refs|“Test.Epict.” 1.14, also κατεσκέαξα “Africa Italiana” 1.330 (from Cyrene): _Boeotian dialect_}} _aorist_ _infinitive_ **-σκευάττη** {{Refs|“SIG” 1185.13}} (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC): _perfect_ **-εσκεύᾰκα** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.30}}: _future_ _middle_ 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _singular_ **-ᾶται** {{Refs|“SIG” 1015.28}} (Halic.): _Doric dialect_ _aorist_ _middle_ **-εσκευαξάμην** {{Refs|“Test.Epict.” 1.9}}:—**equip, furnish fully with.. **, [πᾶσι] κ. τὸ πλοῖον **with** all appliances, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.194:}} —_middle_, τοὺς ἵππους Χαλκοῖς.. προβλήμασι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 6.1.51:}} —frequently in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἱρὸν θησαυροῖσί τε καὶ ἀναθήμασι κατεσκευασμένον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.33, compare 2.44; κατασκευὴ Χρυσῶ τε καὶ ἀργύρω κατεσκ. [prev\. author] 9.82; οἷς ἡ Χώρα κατεσκεύασται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91}}.
**2** without _dative_, **furnish, equip fully**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τὴν Χώραν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 1.9.19; κ. τινὰ ἐπὶ στρατιάν [prev\. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.3}}]; [ἐλέφαντας] {{Refs|κ. πρὸς τὴν πολεμικὴν Χρείαν “OGI” 54.12}} (Adule, 3rd c.BC):—_middle_, κ. τοὺς ὄνους **having got his** asses **ready**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ, etc.:}} —_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τῆς Ἀντάνδρου μελλούσης -σκευάζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.75, compare 8.24; ἔργα -ασμένα}} **cultivated** farms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus}} 4; of persons, **to be under treatment**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Lib.” p.3 O., +others}}
**3** **construct, build**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|γέφυραν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186}} (_passive_); [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|διδασκαλεῖον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.11; πόλιν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 557d; γυμνάσια [prev\. author] “Lg.” 761c}}]; ἱερὰ θυσίας τε αὑτοῖς κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Criti.” 113c; ἐπιτείχισμ᾽ ἐπὶ τὴν Ἀττικήν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.71}}: generally, **prepare, arrange, establish**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|κ. δημοκρατίαν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.36; δύναμιν τῇ πόλει Andocides Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 3.39; συμπόσιον Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 363c; ἰσότητα τῆς οὐσίας [prev\. author] “Lg.” 684d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pol.” 1265a39; ὀλιγαρχίαν [prev\. author] “Ath.” 37.1; ναύτας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.36}}]; κ. τινὰς μελέτῃ **train** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 8.1.43}}, etc.; **turn out**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|πολιτικούς 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.264 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others:}} —_middle_, κατασκευάζεσθαι ναυμαχίαν **prepare** it, **make ready for** it, variant for{παρασκ-} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.85}}; **make for oneself**, especially **build** a house **and furnish** it, opposed to {{Refs|ἀνασκευάζομαι [prev\. author] 1.93, 2.17}}; **unpack**, opposed to ἀνασκ., [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 8.5.2; κ. ἐρημίαν αὑτῶ Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 730c}}], etc.; κ. τράπεζαν **set up** a bank, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator “fragment” 66; κατασκευάζομαι τέχνην μυρεψικήν}} I **am setting up** as a perfumer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “fragment” 1.2; τοὺς ἐγγυτάτω τῆς ἀγορᾶς κατεσκευασμένους [prev\. author] 24.20}}; [πρόσοδον] οὐ μικρὰν κατεσκευάσαντο **made themselves** a good [income], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.61, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.11.}}
**4** of fraudulent transactions, **fabricate, trump up**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|πρόφασιν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.17; τὸ ἀπόρρητον κατασκευάσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.6; λιποταξίου γραφὴν κατεσκεύασεν [prev\. author] 21.103, compare92; Χρέα ψευδῆ [prev\. author] 42.30, compare 45.22 (_passive_)}}]; of persons, **suborn**, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|λογοποιούς Dinarchus Orator [{4-3rd c.BC}] 1.35}}]; **set up**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|ἢ.. ἐπιτίθενται αὐτοὶ ἢ κατασκευάζουσιν ἕτερον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306a1}}; οἱ κατεσκευασμένοι τῶν Θετταλῶν men **prepared for the purpose**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.151; κατεσκ. δανεισταί [prev\. author] 42.28}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs|τὸν ἀνεψιὸν.. κατεσκεύασεν ἀμφισβητεῖν [prev\. author] 55.1}}.
**5** with _double_ _accusative_, **make, render**, [φρούρια] [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|κ. ὡς ἐχυρώτατα Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.17}}]; ἀριστερὰ δεξιῶν ἀσθενέστερα κ, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 795a; φοβερὸν κ. τὸ αὐτόχειρα γενέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.158; ἀνομοθέτητον ἑαυτῶ τὸν βίον Duris Epigrammaticus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 10 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; κ. τινὰ τοιοῦτον.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1380a2}}] (also with _adverb_, πρὸς ἑαυτὸν κ. εὖ τὸν ἀκροατήν _render_ the audience favourably disposed towards oneself, {{Refs|1419b11).}}
**6** **represent as** so and so, κ. τινὰς παροίνους, ὑβριστάς, ἀγνώμονας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.14, compare 45.82; εἰ μὴ Γοργίαν Νέστορά τινα κατασκευάζεις}} unless you **make out** a Gorgias **to be** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Nestor Epigrammaticus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.” 261c.}}
**7** in argument, **maintain, prove**, τῶν ἐν Εὐβοίᾳ πραγμάτων.. ὡς ἐγὼ αἴτιός εἰμι, κατεσκεύαζε **tried to make out** that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.110; κ. ὅτι.. 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.15.14, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “P.” 1.32; κ. τῶ λόγῳ}} **establish** a proposition by reasoning, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Damianus Opticus “Opt.” 5; διὰ λόγου -σκευασθήσεται 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 6}}.
**8** in Logic, **construct** a positive argument, opposed to ἀναιρέω, ἀνασκευάζω (of negative arguments), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh.” 1401b3, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1036b, etc.: Philos., κ. τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἰδέαν}} **construct, postulate**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “EN” 1096a19, compare “Metaphorical” 984b25, +others}}
**9** Geometry texts, **construct**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 5.7 (_passive_), 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Sph.Cyl.” 2.6 (_passive_)}}; **solve by a construction**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|πρόβλημα 3rd c.AD: Pappus Mathematicus 54.25}}.
**10** Rhet., **frame**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|ὀνόματα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Comp.” 16}}; **elaborate**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|κατεσκεύασται τὸ δοκοῦν εἶναι ἀφελές [prev\. author] “Is.” 7; λόγος κατεσκευασμένος 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.6}}.
**11** _absolutely_ in _middle_, **prepare oneself** or **make ready for** doing, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ὡς πολεμήσοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7; ὡς οἰκήσων Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 3.2.24; ὡς εἰς μάχην 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.21.14}}].
**12** _passive_, of disease, **to become established**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|-σκευαζομένου τοῦ πάθους 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.332}}.

2686:
** κατασοφ-ίζομαι**,
**outwit by sophisms** or **fallacies**, with _accusative_ _person_, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Exo.1.10, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 1.1, etc.; ταῖς εὑρησιλογίαις κ. τὴν δύναμιν τῆς πεπρωμένης 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.116; τὸν νόμον διὰ τῆς ἑαυτοῦ κακουργίας κ. 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 72.5}}: with _genitive_, Ael.in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristophanes Byzantinus Philosophus “Epit.” 58.6:}} —also as _passive_, **to be outwitted**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.80c, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in SE” 43.22, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 16.2, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 17.1.}}
**2** κ. τι περί τινων **evade by quibbling**, {{Refs|“CIG”(add.)4224d10 (from Anticragus).}}
**3** **falsify**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “AJ” 8.15.5.}}

2688:
** κατάστημα**, ατος, τό,
later **κατάστεμα** {{LXX|LXX.3Mac.5.45}}:— **condition, state**, not necessarily permanent:
**1** bodily or mental **condition**, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|τὸ εὐσταθὲς σαρκὸς κ. Epicurus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “fragment” 68, 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 5, compare 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 29, 2nd c.AD: Aspasius Philosophus “in EN” 143.22; τὸ κατὰ φύσιν καὶ οἰκεῖον κ. 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Alex.Praef.”, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.36; τῆς ψυχῆς [prev. work] 39; τὸ κατὰ μέθην κ. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.38e; κ. μανιῶδες Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum}}] [prev. cited]
**2** **weather**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Diocl.fragment 30, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 3.1; τὸ θερινὸν κ. Ps.- 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 12; κ. χειμέριον 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 5.12.3; αἰθρίου ὄντος τοῦ κ. 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Praef.” 6, compare 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.1, Sabin. cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 9.15.1}}; **direction of wind**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|νοτίου τοῦ κ. ὄντος 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum libros commentaria - pars” 47.2}}; **time, season**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|κ. νυκτερινόν 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Synt.” 198.27; τὰ ἐνιαύσια κ. 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 93}}.
**3** **demeanour, behaviour, Ep. Tit.**[{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2.3, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Abst.” 4.6; τὸ σύνηθες κ. Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Marcellus” 23; ἀτρεμαῖον κ. 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.7.5}}]; τὸ τῆς εὐσεβείας, τῆς ἀρετῆς κ., Aristeas {{Refs|210, 278; τὸ μέσον κ. [prev\. author] 122}}.
**4** **political condition, constitution**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|τὸ Λακωνικὸν κ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.50.2, compare “OGI” 669.3 (Egypt, 1st c.AD).}}
**5** generally, **state of the case, state of affairs**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Pron.” 25.18.}}
**6** Astrology texts, **position** of the heavens, {{Refs|Vett. Val. 71.23.}}
**7** **physical constitution**, τὸ κοσμικὸν κ., i.e. the four elements and four winds, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev\. author] 175.10; ἐν στερεῷ τινι καὶ οὐσιώδει κ. Damascius Philosophus [{5-6th c.AD}] “de Principiis” 124}}]; of the Intelligible World, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work] 119; ὑποστάσεως κατάστημα Simplicius Philosophus [6th c.AD] “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 232.1}}.

2689:
** καταστολ-ή**, ἡ,
**equipment, dress**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.1Tim.2.9, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “BJ” 2.8.4: _metaphorically_, κ. δόξης Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Isa.61.3}}.
**II** **putting down, checking**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.94}}; **reduction, subjugation**, Ηβαΐδος Wilcken {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Chr.” 12.15 (1st c.BC).}}
**II.2** **modesty, reserve**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Decent.” 5, 8}}; **moderation**, κ. περιβολῆς in dress, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Per.” 5: _absolutely_}}, **dignity, restraint**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|κ. καὶ εὐσχημοσύνη “Inscription Prien.” 109.186 (2nd c.BC), cf. Aristeas 284; ἡ τοῦ βίου σώφρων κ. “IGRom.” 4.1756.66}} (Sardes, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC), compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epict.” 2.10.15, 21.11, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Abst.” 4.6.}}
**II.3** **conclusion, 'finale', Mim.Oxy.**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|413.95; δράματος Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1203}}; **remission**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|τῆς ὀδύνης 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 74}}.

2690:
** καταστρέφω**,
_perfect_ κατέστραφα (_transitive_) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.11.2:}} —_passive_, _future_ - [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|στρᾰφήσομαι Dio Cassius Historicus [{2-3rd c.AD}] 42.42}}]: _perfect_ _imperative_ [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|κατεστρέφθω Epicurus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}]}}] (see. below): _pluperfect_ 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _singular_ -έστραπτο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus (see. below); 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _plural_ -εστράφατο 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.5}}:— **turn down, trample on**, {{Refs|ποσσί “hymnus ad Apollinem” 73}}; **turn the soil**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oec.” 17.10; κάνθαρον κ.}} **turn** it **upside down**, so as to drain it, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 115, compare 3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus Comedy texts 1.33, LXX.2Ki.21.13; κατεστραμμένῳ τῷ ὀστράκῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 622b8}}.
**II** **upset, overturn**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τὴν πόλιν κ. Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Equites” 274; τὰς εἰκόνας 3rd c.AD: Diogenes Laertius 5.82}}]; **ruin, undo**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|βίον καὶ τέκνα καὶ πόλεις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.11.2}}; τινα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“AP” 11.163 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus):}} —_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τὰ προάστεια κατέστραπτο 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.4.8}}.
**II.2** _middle_, **subject to oneself, subdue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|πολέμῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64, compare 71, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.244, etc.; νόσον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 477}}; τοὺς μὲν κατεστρέψατο ἐς φόρου ἀπαγωγήν **subdued and made** them tributary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs|Ἰωνίην κατεστρέψατο δασμοφόρον εἶναι [prev\. author] 7.51}}.
**II.3** _passive_, in _aorist_ and _perfect_, **to be subdued**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 1.130, 68}}: _pluperfect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.29}}: c._infinitive_, ἀκούειν σοῦ κατέστραμμαι **am constrained** to hear, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” 956}}: _perfect_ _passive_ also in sense of _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171; πάντα κατέστραπται καὶ ἔχει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 5.2.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.21.}}
**III** of a floating solid, **right itself**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Fluit.” 2.9 (_passive_).}}
**III.b** _intransitive_, **return**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|εἰς ταὐτόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 921a26, compare “Mech.” 856b17.}}
**IV** **turn round, direct**, [καταπάλτην] **train** it on the enemy, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus “Bel.82.14”; especially towards an end, ποῖ καταστρέφεις λόγων τελευτή; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pers.” 787; οὕτω κατέστρεψεν ἡ τύχη ταῦτα, ὥστ᾽ ἐναντία γενέσθαι τοῖς προσδοκωμένοις Dinarchus Orator [{4-3rd c.BC}] 1.32}}]; κατέστρεψεν εἰς φιλανθρωπίαν τοὺς λόγους **guided** the conversations **to** a friendly **end**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.39}}: hence, **bring to an end**, κ. τὴν βίβλον, τὸν λόγον, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.118.10, 22.9.4 (_passive_, ταῦτα μὲν αὐτοῦ κατεστρέφθω Epicurus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.6); especially κ. τὸν βίον Cebes 10, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “NA” 13.21, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Thes.” 19,etc.; ὑπὸ τῶν πολεμίων [prev\. author] “Comp. Sol.Publ.” 1}}]: _absolutely_, **come to an end, close**, [{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.2.1; τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ -στρέφοντος Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Caesar” 51}}]; especially **end life, die**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Ep.” 3p.61U., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Them.” 31, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “An.” 7.3.1, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.8.10; κ. εἰς ἀπώλειαν}} **end** in.., [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.70; τοὺς λόγους ἐπὶ τὰ πράγματα -στρέφειν οἰόμενος Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Philopoemen” 4}}]; ἡ ἡμέρα κ. εἰς ὥραν δεκάτην **inclines** towards.., {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Sull.” 29.}}
**IV.2** Rhet., _metaphorically_, λέξις κατεστραμμένη **periodic** style, opposed to εἰρομένη, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh.” 1409a26, cf. Demetrius Astrologus “Eloc.” 12, 21.}}
**V** **screw** or **stretch tight**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|αἱ κατεστραμμέναι χορδαί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 803a28}}.

2692:
** καταστροφ-ή**, ἡ,
**overturning**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|θεσμίων Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Eumenides” 490}}] (Lyric poetry, _plural_).
**2** **subjugation, reduction**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, 92, etc.; καταστροφὴν ποιήσασθαί τινος [prev\. author] 6.27; ἐπὶ Λιβύων καταστροφῇ πέμπεσθαι [prev\. author] 4.167; ἐπ᾽ ἄλλων καταστροφῇ ἐξιέναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.15}}: _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|καταστροφαὶ ἐθνῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.255 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**3** **return** of vibrating string to axial position, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pr.” 921a17 (_plural_).}}
**II** **end, close, conclusion**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἄνευ δὲ λύπης οὐδαμοῦ καταστροφή Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Supplices” 442}}]; κ. βίου, i.e. death, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” 103, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.54.4, etc.; κ. τοῦ ζῆν Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “Περικειρομένη” 12: without βίου, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sent.” 35; ποία κ. εὐδαιμονεστέρ; 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epict.” 4.10.17; τὸ τέλος αὐτῶν τῆς κ.}}] the event of their **life's end**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.8.6; κ. καὶ συντέλεια τῶν γεγονότων [prev\. author] 3.1.9; κ. λαμβάνειν [prev\. author] 3.47.8}}; τὴν κ. τῆς βίβλου ποιεῖσθαι εἰς.. {{Refs|[prev\. author] 1.13.5}}; in the drama, **dénouement, ending**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 191.19, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Aut.” 22.6, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 60, +others, Euanth. et 4th c.AD: Donatus, Aelius Grammaticus Latinus in “CGF” pp.67,69 K.: _plural_, αἱ κ. τῶν δραμάτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.48.8}}.
**III** **ruin, undoing**, κ. γῆς (of a person) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 548.8.}}
**IV** **crane,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Stud.Pal. 10.259.13 (_plural_, 6th c.AD).}}

2693:
** κατα-στρώννῡμι**,
also **καταστρωννύω** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Job.12.23, Mitteis “Chr.” 31 viii 18 (2nd c.BC)}}: _future_ -στρώσω:—_passive_, _future_ - {{Refs|στρωθήσομαι Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum “Judith” 7.14}}: _aorist_ -εστρώθην (see. below):—**spread out**, κλίνην Hierocl.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|p.63 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}
**II** **spread over, cover**, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|οἶκον.. ῥόδοις Aelianus [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Varia Historia” 9.8}}]:—_passive_, πεδίον νεκρῶν κατεστρώθη **was strewed with**.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.114; σκορπίων κανθήλιον -εστρωμένον 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.26}}.
**III** **lay low**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|δάμαρτα καὶ παῖδ᾽ ἑνὶ κατέστρωσεν βέλει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1000, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 3.3.64:}} —_passive_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|ὡς δὲ Ἕλλησι κατέστρωντο οἱ βάρβαροι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.76, compare 8.53, “NT.1Cor.10.5”.}}
**IV** **layer**, in _passive_ of vines, {{Refs|“Gp.” 5.17.11.}}
**IV.2** βοτάνιον κατὰ τοῦ ἐδάφους -εστρωμένον **prostrate**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.130.}}

2694:
** κατασύρω** [ῡ],
_aorist 1_ κατέσῡρα (see. below):—_middle_, _aorist 1_ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|κατεσϋράμην 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Lerius Historicus 158 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus:}} —_passive_, _aorist 2_ κατεσύρην [ῠ] (see. below):—**draw, pull down**, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.12 (_passive_): usually with a notion of violence, τὰ [ἀεροπόρα] ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ Dio Cassius Historicus [{2-3rd c.AD}] “fragment” 30.4}}]: _metaphorically_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|ἐπιθυμία κ. τὸν ἡνίοχον λογισμόν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.58, compare 1.627 (_passive_)}}: especially **lay waste, ravage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὰς [πόλιας] ὅσας πρότερον οὐ κατέσυραν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.33; κατὰ μὲν ἔσυραν Φάληρον, κατὰ δὲ.. πολλοὺς δήμους [prev\. author] 5.81; ὡς πλείστην τῆς χώρας 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 16.8, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.56.3, +others}}
**2** **drag, carry off**, {{NT+6th c.BC+|λείαν 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Lerius Historicus [same place]; γυναῖκας 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 19; τινὰ πρὸς τὸν κριτήν NT.Luke.12.58}}: _metaphorically_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|τινὰ εἰς ἐκμελῆ πολιτεύματα 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 93.1}}.
**3** **sweep away**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|πελάγη κ. πόλεις 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.142}}:—_passive_, _metaphorically_, σκολιὰ ῥεῖθρα ὑφ᾽ ὧν οἱ πολλοὶ -σύρονται, ὡς τὰ λόγιά φησιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Oracle texts Chald.” cited in 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Ti.” 3.326 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; εἰς τὸ πλῆθος ὑπὸ τοῦ μερισμοῦ καὶ τῆς διαστάσεως τῶν ὄντων [prev\. author] “in Prm.” p.551 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare 4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor “in CA” 19p.461M.}}
**3.b** _passive_, **rush down**, of rivers, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 296, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.13, “Gp.” 5.2.17.}}
**4** **drag out**, οὐρανὸς.. δρόμον ἀΐδιον -σύρων {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Oracle texts Chald.” cited in 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “Pr.” 284.}}
**5** _passive_, **to be reduced**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|σωμάτων λοιμῷ -συρέντων 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 61.19}} (variant{συρέντων}).
**II** **draw down, launch**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|τὸ σκαφίδιον 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.1}}; τοὺς φελλοὺς κ. ὑφάλους, of a net, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}

2698:
** κατατίθημι**,
_future_ -θήσω: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently uses the _Epic dialect_}} _aorist_ forms, _active_ κάτθεμεν, κάτθετε, κάτθεσαν, _infinitive_ κατθέμεν (_Doric dialect_ κατθέμειν probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 71, _Aeolic dialect_ κά (θ) θηκε “Schwyzer” 647a}} (Naucratis, 6th c.BC)), _middle_ κατθέμεθα, κατθέσθην, κατθέμενοι (_singular_ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|κάτθετο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.867); also καταθείομεν}}, _aorist_ _subjunctive_ for καταθῶμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.264; καταθείομαι}}, _aorist_ _subjunctive_ _middle_ for καταθῶμαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.111, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.17}}:—**place, put, lay down**, followed by various Preps., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|κ. ἄρνας ἐπὶ χθονός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.293; κόρυθ᾽ ἐπὶ χθονί 6.473}}; κ. τινὰ ἐν Λυκίης δήμῳ or εἰς Ἰθάκην, **set** him **down in**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|16.683, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.230; τινὰ ἐν λεχέεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.233; τεύχε᾽ ἐς θάλαμον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.166; ἐς μέγαρον ἐπὶ θρόνου 20.96; κλισίην τινὶ παρὰ πυρί 19.55; τι ὑπὸ ζυγά 13.20}}; τι ἐκ καπνοῦ **take down** out of the smoke, {{Refs|16.288, 19.7.}}
**2** **put down, offer as a prize**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἄπυρον κατέθηκε λέβητα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.267, compare 885 (tmesis); κ. ἄεθλα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.91}}; but κ. ἄεθλον **ordain** a contest, {{Refs|19.572; εἰς τὴν ἀγορὰν γράμματα κ.}} **set up** as a public notice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 946d; so also κ. τι ἐς μέσον}} **put** it **down** in the midst, i.e. for common use, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyc.” 547, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ec.” 602; οὐσίαν, χρήματα κ., [prev. work] 855, 871; τὰ ὅπλα εἰς τὸ μέσον Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.14}}]; but ἐς μέσον Πέρσῃσι κ. τὰ πρήγματα **communicate** power to them, **give** them **a common share of** it, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80; ἐς μέσον Κῴοισι κ. τὴν ἀρχήν [prev\. author] 7.164; τὸ αὑτῶν ἔργον ἅπασι κοινὸν κ. Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 369e}}]; κ. εἰς τὸ μέσον or εἰς τὸ κοινόν, **propose** for common discussion, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Phlb.” 14b, “Cra.” 384c.}}
**3** **put down as payment, pay down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.120, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.” 176, “Nu.” 246, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 314b, “Lg.” 921d, etc.; τέλη 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.77; μετοίκιον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.9; τὸ ὄφλημα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.99, compare 151; τὰς συμβολάς 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 26.8}}; **put down as paid** (in accounts), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oec.” 9.8; τί.. τουτοινὶ καταθῶ σοι}}..; what **shall I pay** you for these? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1214}}: generally, **pay, perform** what one has promised, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|νικῶντί γε χάριν κ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.76}}; ἃ δ᾽ ὑπέσχεο ποῖ καταθήσει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” 227 (_anapaest meter_):}} —alsoin _middle_, see.below{{Refs|11.7.}}
**3.b** **dedicate,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|Schwyzer 647a (Naucratis, 6th c.BC), 682 (2) (5th c.AD: Cypria), +others}}
**4** **deposit**, παρακαταθήκην ὲς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. ; ἐνέχυρα “IG” 5(2).344.18 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 3rd c.BC):}} — in this sense usually in _middle_, compare {{Refs|11.4.}}
**4.b** **mortgage,** {{Refs|Leges Gortynensium 6.19, “Test.Epict.” 2.13, etc.}}
**5** **deposit** in the tomb, **bury**, variant in {{NT|NT.Mark.15.46.}}
**6** **sow** seed, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1031.17 (3rd c.AD).}}
**7** κ. ὁδόν **lay down, make** a road, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.90.}}
**8** **dish up, serve**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 71.}}
**9** in late form κατατίθω, **consign**, {{Refs|ἀγγέλοις καταχθονίοις “Tab.Defix.Aud.” 75.1}}.
**II** _middle_, **lay down from oneself, put off, lay aside**, especially of arms, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|τεύχεα.., τὰ μὲν κατέθεντ᾽ ἐπὶ γαίῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.114, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.141 (hence, comically, θυμὸν κατάθου παρὰ τὴν ὀργὴν ὥσπερ ὁπλίτης Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 401); χλαίνας μὲν κατέθεντο κατὰ κλισμούς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.86}}]; ζώναν καταθηκαμένα, of a maiden, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.39; θοἰμάτιον, etc., 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pl.” 926, etc.; τὴν χλαμύδα (of the ἔφηβος) probably in 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 34; τὴν μοναρχίαν}} **lay down**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fab.” 9.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **put an end to, settle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὸν πόλεμον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.264:}} —_passive_, ξυμφορᾶς μετρίως κατατιθεμένης **being arranged** on tolerable terms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20.}}
**II.2.b** **put aside, leave out of the question**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τοὺς ποιητάς Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Protagoras” 348a, compare “Ti.” 59c, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 3; κ. ἐν ἀμελείᾳ}}] **treat** negligently, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 1.4.15.}}
**II.3** **lay down** in a place; of the dead, **bury**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.190; κ. πηδάλιον ὑπὲρ καπνοῦ {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 45; τὰς μαχαίρας ἐνθαδί Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Equites” 489}}]; [τὰ στρώματα] {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev\. author] “Ra.” 166; ὤμοισι κατ᾽ ἄμβροτα θήκατο τεύχη on one's shoulders, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 12.303}}; **put on shore, disembark**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 57; ποῖ δὴ ἡμᾶς ὁ ἀνὴρ -θήσετα; 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caes.” 37: _metaphorically_, πολλὰ αἱ μακραὶ ἁμέραι κατέθεντο λύπας ἐγγυτέρω}} **have brought** them nearer.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” 1216 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.4** **deposit for oneself, lay by, lay up in store** (see.above[{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.4), [γαστέρας] ἐπὶ δόρπῳ for supper, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.45; ἔντεα ἐς θάλαμον 19.17; ὅπλα εἰς τὰς ἄκρας Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.34; βίον {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 601}}]; σμικρὸν ἐπὶ σμικρῷ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work] 361; καρποὺς ἐς φορβήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.202; παραθήκην [prev\. author] 6.73; χρήματα 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 54; θησαυρὸν παισί 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 409; θησαυροὺς ἐν οἴκῳ Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.15; μυρίους δαρεικοὺς εἰς τὸ ἴδιον ἐμοί [prev\. author] “An.” 1.3.3}}]; [σῖτον] **hoarditup** in hope of high prices, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.9.}}
**II.4.b** _metaphorically_, κλέος **lay up store of** glory, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.220, 9.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” 208c; ἀΐδιον δόξαν κ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.87; κ. ἀποστροφήν τινι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 7.6.34}}]: frequently χάριτα or χάριν κατατίθεσθαι τινι or πρός τινα, **lay up a store of** gratitude or favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.41, 7.178, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.61, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.21, etc.; εὐεργεσίαν κ. ἐς βασιλέα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128}} (so in _passive_, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|μεγάλων μοι κατατεθεισῶν ἐς αὐτὸν εὐεργεσιῶν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.6.2, compare 1.4.3); also ἔχθραν καταθέσθαι πρὸς ἐκείνους ὑπὲρ αὐτῶν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.22; κατέθετο μῖσος διπλάσιον τῆς οὐσίας Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 626}}]; but κ. ὀργὴν εἴς τινας **vent** one's fury upon.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyn.” 10.8.}}
**II.5** **deposit in a place of safety**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τοὺς πρέσβεις κατέθεντο εἰς Αἴγιναν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.72; τὴν λείαν ἐς τοὺς Βιθυνούς Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.3.2; κ. εἰς τὸ οἴκημα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.4; οἴκαδε Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Protagoras” 314a; διαθήκην παρά τινι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.27; φιλίαν παρὰ θεούς Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 2.5.8; [Διόνυσον] ἐν Δρακάνῳ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 26.34}}]:—_passive_, of prisoners, ἐν τῷ δεσμωτηρίῳ κατατεθῶσιν Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.63, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 58.1.}}
**II.6** **lay up in memory** or **as a memorial**, χρὴ.. γνώμην ταύτην (ταύτῃ Bgk.) [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|καταθέσθαι 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 717; μνημεῖον παρά τινι Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Theaetetus” 209c}}]; κ. εἰς μνήμην **record, register**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Lg.” 858d; κ. τι ἐς βιβλίον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.2; γνώμην -θέσθαι εἰς μέσον 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 9.4}}.
**II.7** **pay** (compare {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1.3), ἐκφόριον “BGU” 1059.22 (1st c.BC), compare “PTeb.” 329.7 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.7.b** **employ, spend**, τὴν ἀκμὴν.. πρὸς τί κατατιθέμενος on what he is **employing** the prime of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus Comedy texts 13.4; τὸν βίον εἴς τι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.244 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; κ. τὴν σχολὴν εἰς καλόν τι}} **employ one's** leisure in.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.135d; τὴν τοῦ λέγειν δύναμιν εἰς τὴν τῶν ἀδικουμένων βοήθειαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 9.13}}; σπουδὴν-τιθέμενοι {{Refs|Polystr. p.19 W.}}
**II.8** **impose**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ὄνομα Parmenides Poeta Philosophus [{4-5th c.BC}] 19.3, compare 8.39; but μορφὰς κατέθεντο δύο γνώμας ὀνομάζειν}}] **recorded their decision, decided** to name, {{Refs|[prev. work] 53.}}
**II.9** in Law, **depose, aver**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|ἐν ὑπομνήμασι “PLips.” 35.16 (4th c.AD), compare “Codex Just.” 1.5.16.1, etc.}}
**II.9.b** ={συγκατατίθεμαι}, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1261.19.}}
**II.9.c** **make** a testamentary **disposition**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|κ. διαθηκιμαίαν βούλησιν “PMasp.” 151.43, +others (6th c.AD).—Frequently in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and familiar _Attic dialect_; rare in Trag.}}

2699:
** κατατομή**, ἡ,
**incision, notch, groove**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 4.8.10, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX “Je.” 31 (48).37; ἄνευ -τομῆς _uncarved, smooth, Inscriptiones Graecae 12.372.134}}, compare 373.231: _plural_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.67.}}
**II** **part of a theatre**, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Dem.fragment” 3: variously explained as ={ὀρχήστρα} or διάζωμα, “AB” 270, compare 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}]
